%\vspace{-.15in}
\section{Large-Scale FEM Data}
%\vspace{-.1in}

% The development of our neural network framework relies on a large-scale dataset of simulations of finite element (FE) solutions. Specifically, our application concentrates on developing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy surgical simulator. Therefore, our FE simulations focus on modeling linear and non-linear tissue responses of the initial surgical step of lifting the liver lobes to expose the gallbladder.

Our neural network framework trains on a large FE simulation dataset, focused on lifting liver lobes to expose the gallbladder during laparoscopic cholecystectomy. We construct an FE model from an abdominal CT image and its segmentation masks, converting them into a 3D linear tetrahedral mesh with 10,400 nodes and 44,045 tetrahedra using `gmsh' \cite{gmsh2009reference}.

Interactions of the liver and gallbladder with surrounding organs are modeled by prescribing boundary conditions. Those boundary conditions restrict regions of the liver’s exposed surface that are considered in the far field from the interaction location, as well as the distal end of the cystic ducts, arteries, and veins. FE simulations are solved using the open source getFEM package \cite{getFEM2020}.


% % \begin{figure}[htb]
% % \begin{center}
% % \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{}}
% % \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{}}
% % \end{center}
% % \caption{(a) Segmentation containing critical anatomical structures from CT image; (b) Finite element model mesh (10302 nodes and 46384 elements).}
% % \label{fig:fem_model} 
% % \end{figure}

% The boundary conditions were carefully chosen to mimic the real surgical environment. During the procedure, the liver and gallbladder surfaces interact with the surrounding organs, and prescribing reasonable anatomical boundary conditions is challenging. The bare area of the liver as well as the extreme portions of the cystic ducts, arteries and veins were assumed to be fixed (\( \mathbf{u} = 0 \)).

Assuming a quasi-static environment, our solutions satisfy equilibrium by ensuring that the total potential energy is not only stationary but is also a minimum for permissible displacements, and is given by: $\int_{\Omega} \frac{\boldsymbol{W}(\boldsymbol{E})}{\partial \boldsymbol{\boldsymbol{E}}} : \delta \boldsymbol{E}\, dV + \int_{\Omega} \rho \boldsymbol{g} \cdot \delta \mathbf{u}\, dV = 0$, where $\boldsymbol{W}(\boldsymbol{E})$ is the strain energy density, $\Omega$ is the volume of the model, $\delta \mathbf{u}$ is the variation of the displacement field, $\delta \boldsymbol{E}$ is the variation of the Green-Lagrange strain tensor, $\rho$ is the density of the material (1000 $kg / m^3$), and $\boldsymbol{g}$ is the acceleration due to gravity that points downward along the y-axis (9.81 $m /s^2$). The Green-Lagrange strain tensor $\boldsymbol{E}$ depends on the unknown displacement field $\mathbf{u}$ and is defined as:
% For large displacements, the Green-Lagrange strain tensor $ \boldsymbol{E}$ is a function of the unknown displacement field  $\mathbf{u}$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:deform_tensor}
\boldsymbol{E} = \frac{1}{2} \left(\nabla \mathbf{u} + \nabla \mathbf{u}^T + \nabla \mathbf{u}^T \nabla \mathbf{u} \right).
\end{equation}
%In addition, the Cauchy-Green deformation tensor $\boldsymbol{C}$ can be written as a function of the Green-Lagrange strain tensor as: $\boldsymbol{C} = 2\boldsymbol{E} - \mathbf{I}$, where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity tensor.
In addition, this equation relates to the Cauchy-Green deformation tensor $\boldsymbol{C}$ by: $\boldsymbol{C} = 2\boldsymbol{E} - \mathbf{I}$, where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity tensor.

For our nonlinear simulations, we use a two-parameter Mooney-Rivlin model, where the strain energy density can be written as functions of the Cauchy-Green deformation tensor invariants ($\boldsymbol{I_i(C)}$, with $i=1,2,3$) \cite{Hackett2018}: 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{W} = C_{01} \left( \boldsymbol{I_1(C)}  \boldsymbol{I_3(C)}^\frac{-1}{3}- 3 \right) + C_{10} \left( \boldsymbol{I_1(C)}  \boldsymbol{I_3(C)}^\frac{-2}{3} - 3 \right),
\end{equation}
where $C_{10} = 1.62$ kPa and $C_{01} = 1.97$ kPa are constitutive constants derived from ex-vivo porcine liver experiments \cite{SPIE2025}. 

For our linear simulations, we simulate an elastic response in the absence of gravitational load ($\boldsymbol{g} = 0$). These solutions serve as baseline data for tissue deformation under small elastic input displacements, where the term $\nabla \mathbf{u}^T \nabla \mathbf{u}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:deform_tensor} is neglected. Assuming isotropic material properties, the strain energy density $\boldsymbol{W}$ in terms of shear modulus $\mu$ and Poisson's ratio $\nu$  expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{W}= \left( \frac{\mu \nu}{1 - 2\nu} \right) \mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{E})^2 +\mu \mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{E}^2).
\end{equation}
%\begin{equation}
%\boldsymbol{W}= \left( \mu\frac{\nu}{(1 - 2\nu)} \right) \mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{E})^2 + \mu \mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{E}^2).
%\end{equation}
In these linear simulations, we assign a Poisson's ratio of 0.45 for all organs \cite{chen_youngs_1996}. The shear modulus values are also set as follows: 0.03 kPa for the fascia \cite{akhmanova_physical_2015}, 0.34 kPa for the gallbladder \cite{nisansala_stiffness_2016}, 0.69 kPa for the gallbladder wall and liver \cite{kim_gallbladder_2013}, and 1.07 kPa for the remaining organs \cite{george_influence_2018}.


%\vspace{-.1in}
\subsubsection{Training Distribution, $p$.}
Given a mesh partitioned into regions \( \Omega_i \) informed by cholecystectomy surgery, nodes \( \mathbf{x}_s \) are sampled from a mixture of Generalized von Mises-Fisher (vMF) distributions (i.e., radial densities on the surface mesh) centered in these regions:
\begin{equation}
    p(\mathbf{x}_s) = \sum_{i} g(\Omega_i) f_{\text{vMF}}(\mathbf{x}_s \mid \boldsymbol{\mu}_i, \kappa_i),
    \vspace{-.1in}
    \label{eq:px}
\end{equation}
where \( g(\Omega_i) \) is the prior-informed probability of selecting \( \Omega_i \), $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$ indicates the center of $\Omega_i$, and $\kappa_i$ is the spread of the vMF, which is a function of the size of $\Omega_i$. The signals \( u_s \) are independently sampled from a uniform distribution in a cuboid \( \mathcal{C} \) defined by: $x \in (x_1, x_2), y \in (y_1, y_2), z \in (z_1, z_2)$, with volume: $V = (x_2 - x_1)(y_2 - y_1)(z_2 - z_1).$ This approach ensures that node sampling is spatially structured according to surgical priors while allowing displacements to be isotropic in a bounded range.

For single grasping, ten regions $\Omega_i$ around the liver edge were selected, each with 250 samples. For multiple graspers, six pairwise combinations of five regions $\Omega_i$ were considered, with 30 samples per region. 
The displacement ranges are $x \in (x_1, x_2) = (-90,50)$ mm, $y \in (y_1, y_2) = (-90,50)$ mm, and $z \in (z_1, z_2) = (-60,90)$ mm, corresponding to the cube domain.

%\vspace{-.1in}
\subsubsection{Distribution for Multiple Graspers.} For multiple interactions (e.g., two graspers), we define the joint distribution as \( p(\mathbf{x}_s^1, \mathbf{x}_s^2) = p(\mathbf{x}_s^1) p(\mathbf{x}_s^2 \mid \mathbf{x}_s^1) \), where \( p(\mathbf{x}_s^1) \) follows Eq.~\eqref{eq:px}. To ensure that \( \mathbf{x}_s^2 \) is not sampled from the same region \( \Omega_k \) as \( \mathbf{x}_s^1 \), we modify the region probabilities \( g(\Omega_i) \) by setting \( g(\Omega_k) = 0 \) and adjusting $g(\Omega_{i\neq k})$ based on surgical priors,  and then sampling \( \mathbf{x}_s^2 \) from \( p(\mathbf{x}_s^2 \mid \mathbf{x}_s^1) \).

%\vspace{-.1in}
\subsubsection{Regularization Distribution, $q$.}
For Kelvinlet regularization, interaction points are sampled uniformly from the surface nodes. Initial displacements are then drawn uniformly from a cone centered around the surface normal at \( \mathbf{x}_s \), with a maximum opening angle of \( \alpha_{\max} \).


%\vspace{-.2in}
\section{Texture Rendering for Realistic Simulations}
%\vspace{-.1in}
Our visualization pipeline combines real-time tissue deformation and high-fidelity rendering to create a realistic simulation (see Figure \ref{fig:teaser} and the supplementary videos). In a preprocessing stage, surface meshes are extracted from the segmented high-resolution volumetric mesh, assigned texture coordinates, and enriched with procedurally generated textures using Worley noise 
\cite{worley1996cellular}. Interpolation weights are computed to map low-resolution deformations to the high-resolution mesh \cite{muller2004interactive}, optimized using a Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) \cite{clark1976hierarchical}, and UV coordinates are transferred to the volumetric mesh. At runtime, the system dynamically updates surface meshes to accommodate added or removed tetrahedral elements, interpolates high-resolution vertex positions based on low-resolution deformations, smooths exposed internal structures, and recalculates surface normals. Rendering employs pre-integrated diffuse shading \cite{penner2018pre}, triplanar texture projection for inner tissue surfaces \cite{geiss2007generating}, and post-processing effects within Unity to achieve realistic tissue appearance. The pipeline achieves near-real-time performance while preserving visual fidelity during dynamic deformations.



%\vspace{-.2in}
\section{Experiments}
%\vspace{-.1in}
\subsubsection{Data.}
\label{sec:data}

Our dataset consists of large-scale FEM simulations of soft tissue deformation. Each node in the mesh has a feature vector: $\mathbf{f} = \left[\mathbf{x}; \mathbf{u}_s; a\right] \in \mathbb{R}^7$. Where $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the undeformed position, $\mathbf{u}_s \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the initial displacement, and $a \in \{0,1\}$ indicates whether the node is actively influenced by external forces. This distinction helps differentiate constrained nodes from passively deforming ones. We create a dataset of 20,800 FEM solutions with 10,400 linear elastic simulations and 10,400 nonlinear material responses. Of these, 5,000 correspond to single manipulations and 5,400 to multiple grasper manipulations. 


%\vspace{-.2in}
\subsubsection{Neural Architectures.}
\label{subsec:models}

We evaluate Kelvinlet-based priors using four neural architectures: U-Net \cite{mendizabal2020simulation}, SAB \cite{lee2019set} (set attention), GraphGPS \cite{rampasek2022GPS} (a Graph Neural Network and Transformer hybrid), and PointNet \cite{qi2016pointnet} (a universal function approximator for sets). We modify PointNet and GraphGPS by reducing layers, U-Net by replacing convolutions with shared MLPs to form a DeepSet, and SAB by using two layers with an MLP regressor head.

Each model is trained separately on two datasets: one with individual graspers and another with multiple graspers interacting. For each dataset, models are trained under three settings: (1) a baseline without physics priors (i.e., evaluating solely the network), (2) residual learning predicting deviations from Kelvinlets, and (3) physics-informed regularization enforcing Kelvinlet constraints.

%\vspace{-.2in}
\subsubsection{Hyperparameters.}
\label{subsec:hyperparameters}

We set shear modulus in Kelvinlet to $\mu = 0.72$ kPa, Poisson’s ratio $\nu = 0.45$, and regularization parameter $\epsilon = 0.05$, to reflect soft tissue properties and ensure numerical stability. For linear and nonlinear simulations we use $\lambda_{\text{reg}} = 1$ and $\lambda_{\text{reg}} = 0.1$, respectively, to emphasize linearity of Kelvinlet.

% For linear simulations, we set $\lambda_{\text{reg}} = 1$, while for nonlinear simulations, we use $\lambda_{\text{reg}} = 0.1$ due to reduced accuracy in the nonlinear case.\\

