\noindent To quantitatively assess model performance, we introduce the \textit{Deformation Capture Mean} (DCM) metric, which measures how well a predicted deformation field approximates the ground-truth displacement field. The DCM is computed as:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DCM} = 100 \times 
    \left( 
    1 - \mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x}_s, \mathbf{u}_s)\sim p}
    \left[ 
    \frac{\left\|\mathbf{u}_{\text{true}} - \hat{\mathbf{u}}\right\|_{L_2(\Omega)}}{\|\mathbf{u}_{\text{true}}\|_{L_2(\Omega)}} 
    \right] 
    \right).
\end{equation}
Tables~\ref{tab:resultsLinear} and~\ref{tab:resultsNonlinear} report DCMs across models and settings. Each experiment runs with three random seeds, reporting mean and standard deviation. Models perform better on linear cases, as nonlinear deformations introduce gravity effects, non-proportional stress-strain relationships, and greater sensitivity to perturbations. Notably, Kelvinlet priors consistently improve DCMs, despite being derived for linear elasticity, showing their effectiveness even in nonlinear regimes.

% In the linear case, Kelvinlet priors consistently improve the DCM metric across all models, highlighting their effectiveness in capturing deformation patterns. When extending to the more complex nonlinear simulations with gravity, overall accuracy decreases.  However, despite Kelvinlets being derived for linear elasticity, they still enhance performance, demonstrating their utility as a strong prior even in more challenging deformation regimes. In the multiple grasper scenario, the model must nonlinearly combine their effects rather than relying on a naive superposition, making the task more challenging and leading to lower DCM scores compared to the single grasper case.

