\subsection{Annealing Results} \label{sec:annealing}
\vspace{-3pt}
As described in \S\ref{training}, we demonstrate the benefit of annealing in Tab.~\ref{tab:annealing}. The experimental setups are in \S\ref{sec:setup}. Our observations are as follows:
(1) With both data compositions, we achieve improvement over the baseline and the continue training counterpart, which validates the benefit of annealing\footnote{The only exception is for out-of-domain ASR of \texttt{Opt-B}. Continue training setup means continuing the training without learning rate decay and data composition change for the same number of updates.}.
(2) The out-domain evaluation results imply the improvement is in general rather than adapting to the annealing data domain.
(3) Both long-form ASR and TTS become feasible after annealing, thanks to the inclusion of long-form training data. %\footnote{
% However, the absolute WERs of long-form TTS are still poor. Combining our observation in Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}, we believe TTS is more challenging than ASR in this mixed multi-modal training scheme.
% /}.
(4) Finally, although we see the benefits of annealing in both trials, the exact improvements can be significantly different (e.g., 15.5\% vs. 12.7\% in out-domain TTS). This observation suggests the importance of curating high-quality data composition during the annealing stage.

% Both ASR-focus annealing and TTS-focus annealing achieve considerable improvement, with more improvement over the corresponding task.
% The out-domain evaluation indicates that the improvement achieved by annealing is for the general model performance rather than a result of domain adaptation over the annealing data domain. 
% Compared with the naive continued training for more steps, the annealing achieves much more improvement, which implies the importance of adopting high-quality data during the annealing stage. This is further verified by the text performance: the MMLU score only shows marginal improvement since the text corpus quality does not change during annealing. 
% Finally, we show that long-form ASR becomes feasible with this annealing stage. The long-form TTS task remains challenging, even though it makes a significant improvement over the baseline. Combining our observation in Fig.\ref{fig:scaling}, we believe TTS is more difficult than ASR in this mixed multi-modal training scheme from multiple aspects.



\vspace{-5pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-5pt}
This paper presents OpusLM, a family of pre-trained speech language models that achieve competitive performance on speech recognition, speech synthesis, and text. The code, data, checkpoints, and training logs are released to support open speech language model research in the community.

