When no external oxygen is able to enter the concrete, the corrosion reaction is much more able to sustain itself under these partially saturated conditions. As the corrosion reaction is close to self-sufficiency, even after $28\;\text{days}$ there is still oxygen remaining within the concrete, and the hydrogen reaction current is only reduced to $3\;\mu\text{A}$. Furthermore, the area over which this hydrogen reaction occurs does not significantly differ from the case where oxygen is able to enter. Consequently, under these partially saturated conditions, the corrosion rate observed is higher compared to when the concrete is fully saturated. While similar to the fully saturated conditions, this corrosion will eventually stop, only a slight reduction in corrosion rate is observed, see \cref{fig:Sw025_noox_timeseries}. 

