We now give some intuition for  the upper bound to $\E{N}$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:en-ub1}(b).
%\eqnref{en-ub2}. 
For $\alpha\gg\lambda$, each update is tagged with zero or one reads. As $\alpha\to\infty$, an untagged update expires in expected time $1/\alpha\to 0$, as it is replaced by the next update.  On the other hand, a tagged update enters a grace period with duration corresponding to the exponential $(\mu)$ service time required by its read. Hence tagged updates have a one-to-one correspondence with the reads in the system. The number of tagged updates is described by the M/M/$\infty$ queue process with arrival rate $\lambda$ and service rate $\mu$ that characterizes the number of reads in the system. 
Therefore, in the limiting case of $\alpha\to\infty$, the number $N'$ of tagged updates in the system follows a Poisson distribution
\begin{align}
    \prob{N'=n} = \frac{(\lambda/ \mu)^n e^{-\lambda / \mu}}{n!}, \quad n \geq 0.
\end{align} 
Furthermore, there is always one untagged update that is perpetually being replaced. 
Hence, the number of updates in the system is $N=1+N'$ and the average number of updates holding a read lock is 
%\begin{equation}
    $\E{N}=\E{1+N'} = 1 + {\lambda}/{\mu}$.
%\end{equation}


\section{Conclusion}
In this work, we explored the trade-off between memory footprint and update age in the context of RCU, particularly relevant for applications with sizable updates operating within the constraints of memory-constrained mobile devices. 
The central question revolves around whether frequent updating 
%leads to an infinite number of active updates, posing a potential challenge of 
can induce excessive memory consumption. 
Theorem~\ref{thm:RCU} provides a reassuring finding — given finite average service/read time $1/\mu$  and read request rate $\lambda$, the average number of updates in the system is finite.
% Given the insight in the theorem, this underscores that memory should be sized in proportion to the read requests generated in a given time or to limit the write rate.

One way the memory usage can be regulated is by limiting the update rate $\alpha$ of the writer, albeit at the expense of high AoI. 
Alternatively, the memory footprint can be reduced 
%improving access to memory systems can be achieved 
by controlling the read request rate $\lambda$. 
% The alternative is to limit the read request rate by providing improved access to memory systems. 
% In scenarios with memory constraints, a practical approach is to control the rate at which read requests are generated by processes that access the memory. 
Consider a setting in which an application (such as SLAM) processes an update in a number of modules that can be executed either concurrently or  sequentially.  
%In scenarios with memory limitations, a practical solution involves restricting the concurrent operations of the update processing application.
%sequential, where the execution of some processes becomes sequential. 
In this setting, sequential operation
% , as opposed to concurrent, 
can effectively reduce the read request rate $\lambda$ through various methods.
For example, the modules may generate individual read requests,  but the sequential execution of the modules will slow the overall update processing rate. In an alternate approach that utilizes local copies, the application makes a single read to store the current data item  in a local copy. This local copy is then utilized by  the subsequent module executions. Although a caveat can be that for large objects in the memory, the reads take longer. If a process maintains a local copy, subsequent steps circumvent this read latency, but the data used will be stale if the main memory has been updated. 

While this work analyzes the tradeoff between the memory footprint and the age of updates in the memory, timeliness analysis of these proposals  for structuring the execution of the updating application is likely to be application-specific and remains as future work. 

% Lastly, while our study focused on RCU, we acknowledge that alternative synchronization primitives might offer enhanced performance in managing status updates.
% Future research can extend this analysis to explore such alternatives.

% The challenge becomes more pronounced when dealing with multiple source updates stored concurrently, where readers must access different updates. Each source update may have distinct timeliness requirements, further complicating the memory-age trade-off, and the future work should incorporate these details into the system model.



