Here is an intuitive description of our method. After performing the usual reductions, we add an artificial loop to every vertex, i.e., we put ones along the diagonal of the adjacency matrix. We then Sinkhorn balance the resulting matrix and select the vertex corresponding to the smallest diagonal entry. 

The $i,j^{th}$ entry of the Sinkhorn balanced adjacency matrix approximates the percentage of DCUs that contain the arc from vertex $i$ to vertex $j$. We remove the selected vertex from the digraph and add it to our FVS. We repeat the steps until no cycles remain. This generates an FVS. We finish by removing redundant vertices. These ideas will be made more precise in the rest of the paper.

\begin{center}
\begin{algorithm}[h!]
  \DontPrintSemicolon
  \KwData{A Digraph $G=(V,U)$}
  \KwResult{An FVS $S$}
  \Begin{
    $H \longleftarrow G$\;
    $S \longleftarrow \emptyset$\;
    $LL\_graph\_reductions(H,S)$\;
    $L \longleftarrow get\_SCC(H)$\;
    \While{ $|L| \neq 0$}{
      remove $g$ from $L$\;
      $v \longleftarrow Sinkhorn\_selection(g)$ \;
      remove $v$ from $g$ \;
      $S \longleftarrow S+\{v\}$ \;
      $LL\_reductions(g,S)$ \;	
      $L \longleftarrow get\_SCC(g) + L$\;
    }
    $S \longleftarrow remove\_redundant\_nodes(G,S)$\;
    return $S$
  }
  \caption{FVS\_SH\_Del\label{fvs_sh_del}}
\end{algorithm}
\end{center}
