\begin{abstract}
Recently, Ko\c{c} proposed a neat and efficient algorithm for computing 
$x = a^{-1} \pmod {p^k}$ for a prime $p$ based on the
exact solution of linear equations using $p$-adic expansions. The algorithm 
requires only addition and right shift per step.
In this paper, we design an algorithm that computes $x = a^{-1} \pmod {n^k}$ for any
integer $n>1$. The algorithm has a motivation from the schoolbook multiplication and achieves both efficiency and generality.
The greater flexibility of our algorithm is explored by  utilizing the build-in arithmetic of computer architecture,
e.g., $n=2^{64}$, and experimental results show significant improvements. 
This paper also contains some results on modular inverse based on an alternative proof of correctness of Ko\c{c} algorithm. 

{\bf Key words:} Multiplicative inverse, Modular arithmetic, Schoolbook multiplication.

% MSC(2020) 11Y16, 68W01
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Modular arithmetic is a basic tool in modern computation. Its efficiency impacts heavily on applications in cryptography and communication. In practice, modular multiplication is of the most concern with respect to the cost of computation. There are several modern methods for achieving efficient modular multiplication. The famous Montgomery reduction algorithm  \cite{Mont}
makes a very clever use of the fact that integer division (and modulation) by a power of $2$ is trivial.
The algorithm works on integers with Montgomery representation and requires to compute inverse of an
odd integer (the modulus) modulo the special number $R=2^k$. The computational task of computing inversion of an odd integer modulo a power of $2$ is often seen in other scenarios, e.g. \cite{XZDDF}.


It is expected that the calculation of an inverse modulo a power of $2$ is easier than general case. Indeed 
there have been several surprisingly simple and efficient methods designed for this purpose.  
In \cite{DK},  Duss\'e and Kaliski 
gave an efficient algorithm for computing the inverse by modifying
the extended euclidean algorithm. Arazi and Qi introduce several algorithms to compute 
$x=a^{-1}\pmod{2^k}$ for an odd integer $a>0$ in \cite{AQ} with multiplication being eliminated from the computation.
Dumas \cite{Dum} observed that the idea of Hensel lifting can be used to 
create an iterative formula
for computing $a^{-1}\pmod{p^k}$ for prime number $p$ and $k=2^s$, with $p\not| a$.
Recently in  \cite{Koc},  Ko\c{c} proposed a neat and efficient algorithm for computing $x = a^{-1} \pmod {p^k}$ for a prime number $p$ and $k>0$ \cite{Koc}.
This algorithm is based  on the exact solution of linear equations using $p$-adic expansions.
Nice features of Ko\c{c} algorithm include that only addition (subtraction)\footnote{The algorithm does require multiplications of a digit with a number.} and right shifting (dividing by $p$) are used in each step. We would also like to mention that an inverse modulo power of $2$
algorithm proposed by Hurchalla \cite{Hur} gives the best performance when within the bit size of instruction set of an architecture 
(e.g., the modulus is $2^{64}$).

The main purpose of this paper is to design an efficient and flexible algorithm  that 
computes 
\[
x = a^{-1} \pmod {n^k}
\] for any integers $n>1$ and $k>0$. 

The motivation of our algorithm is the schoolbook multiplication. The formulation of carries in 
computation makes the use of right shifting (dividing by $n$) appear natural. Besides its generality, our algorithm
also has a slight improvement over Ko\c{c} algorithm in terms of the number of operations used. 

However, our algorithm is flexible in that $n>1$ can be arbitrary, 
as long as the number $a$ to be inverted satisfies $\gcd(a,n)=1$. 
A useful application is to set $n=2^{64}$, so one can compute modulo inverse of an odd integer of the form
\[
a = a_0+a_12^{64}+a_2(2^{64})^2+\cdots+a_{k-1}(2^{64})^{k-1}, \ 0\le a_i<2^{64},
\]
to well utilize the build-in arithmetic of a $64$-bit computer. Our 
experiment show that the speed improvement compared to the existing methods is very 
significant.


The contents of the rest of this paper is arranged as follows:
Section 2 describes Ko\c{c}'s algorithm for inversion modulo $p^k$, with an alternative proof of its correctness and some further discussion to
specify several useful properties of the method. In section 3,  the method of inverse modulo a general power is presented, together with its elementary motivation and formulation, as well as a discussion of its performance.
Section 4 summarizes the paper .

\section{Ko\c{c}'s Algorithm for Inversion Mod $p^k$}
In \cite{Koc}, Ko\c{c} proposed a method for computing inverse with a modulus being a prime power. Let us fix a prime $p$ and an integer $k>0$.  Given an integer $a>0$ such that $p\not| a$, then $x=a^{-1}\pmod {p^k}$ exists. Since $0< x <p^k$, it can be uniquely written as  a $p$-adic form
\[
x=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} X_ip^i = X_0+X_1p+X_2p^2+\cdots + X_{k-1}p^{k-1}
\]
with digits $0\le X_i<p$.  Ko\c{c} algorithm executes a loop of length $k$ and produce digits $X_0, X_1, \cdots, X_{k-1}$ one by one. An initial computation of 
$c = a^{-1} \pmod{p}$ is needed. Note that the number $c<p$ is of the size as a digit.

\begin{algorithm}
	\caption{Ko\c{c} Algorithm
		\label{alg:Koc}}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\Require{A prime $p$, and an integer $k>0$; integer $0<a<p^k$ with $\gcd(p,a)=1$}
		\Ensure{$a^{-1} \pmod{p^k}$}
		%\Statex
		\Function{ModInverse}{$a, p^k$}
		\Let{$c$}{$a^{-1}\pmod p$}
		\Let{$b_0$}{$1$}
		\For{$i = 0$ to $k-1$}
		\Let{$X_i$}{$cb_i \pmod p$}
		\Let{$b_{i+1}$}{$\frac{b_i-a  X_i}p $}
		\EndFor
		\State \Return{$x=(X_{k-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_p$}
		\EndFunction
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
In each step of the algorithm,  essentially one subtraction is used. There are also two multiplications of $b_ic$ and $a  X_i$, but the multipliers $c$ and $X_i$ are numbers less than $p$.  The right shifting $\frac{b_i-a  X_i}p $ turns the operation of getting $X_i$ into finding the least significant digit of $cb_i $ .

We shall provide a proof of the correctness of the algorithm  \ref{alg:Koc} 
which is  entirely different from that in \cite{Koc}. This proof will serve the purpose of
revealing some interesting properties of the computation. 
 \begin{prop} Algorithm  \ref{alg:Koc} is correct.
 \end{prop}
 \begin{proof}
 We first note that the least significant digit of $ax$ is $1$ and digits of indeces $1$ to $k-1$ are all zero. So the line $6$ becomes $pb_{i+1} = b_i-aX_i$. Therefore we have the 
 implication
\begin{equation}\label{eq:impl}
\left\{\begin{array}{lll}b_0  &=& 1\\
 	pb_1 &=& b_0-aX_0\\
 	pb_2  &=& b_1-aX_1\\
 	pb_3  &=& b_2-aX_2\\
 	 & &\cdots \\
 	pb_{k-1}  &=& b_{k-2}-aX_{k-2}\\
 	pb_k  &=& b_{k-1}-aX_{k-1}
 	\end{array}
 	\right.
 	\Longrightarrow 
 \left\{	 \begin{array}{lll}b_0  &=& 1\\
 		pb_1 &=& b_0-aX_0\\
 		p^2b_2  &=& pb_1-aX_1p\\
 		p^3b_3  &=& p^2b_2-aX_2p^2\\
 		& &\cdots \\
 		p^{k-1}b_{k-1}  &=& p^{k-2}b_{k-2}-aX_{k-2}p^{k-2}\\
 		p^kb_k  &=& p^{k-1}b_{k-1}-aX_{k-1}p^{k-1}
 	\end{array}
 \right.
\end{equation}
Summing up the equalities on the right, one gets 
\[
p^k b_k = 1-aX_0-aX_1p-\cdots -aX_{k-2}p^{k-2}-aX_{k-1}p^{k-1}=1-ax,
\]
namely, 
\[
ax = 1+(-b_k)p^k.
\]
\end{proof}
It is remarked that more useful information can be revealed from this proof. To be more specific,
we have the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item For each  $s=1,2,\cdots, k-1$,  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ainvmodps}
			(X_{s-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_p = a^{-1}\pmod {p^s}.
\end{equation}
	This can be checked by adding the first $s$ equalities on the right of (\ref{eq:impl}) in the proof, we get
\begin{eqnarray*}
	p^s b_s &=& 1-aX_0-aX_1p-\cdots -aX_{k-2}p^{k-2}-aX_{s-1}p^{s-1}\\
	&=&1-a\big(X_0+X_1p+\cdots +X_{k-2}p^{k-2}+X_{s-1}p^{s-1}\big),
\end{eqnarray*}
	so $(X_{s-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_p=a^{-1}\pmod {p^s}$.

	It is noted that this has been illustrated by the example of computing $12^{-1} \pmod {5^5}$ in \cite{Koc}.
	\item For each  $s=1,2,\cdots, k$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:psinvmoda}
	(p^s)^{-1} \pmod a =a+b_s.
\end{equation}	
In fact, denoting $u=(X_{s-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_p$, we have $p^sb_s+au=1$ and $|b_s|<a$. Since
\[
(a+b_s)p^s = 1+(p^s-u)a.
\]
and $b_s<0$, we see that $(p^s)^{-1} \pmod a =a+b_s.$
\end{enumerate}

For the case of  $p=2$, Ko\c{c} \cite{Koc} gets the following procedure for $a^{-1}\pmod {2^k}$ 
which is significantly more efficient than the existing algorithms (before the time of \cite{Hur})
for small $k$ :
\begin{algorithm}
	\caption{Binary Version of Ko\c{c} Algorithm
		\label{alg:Koc-2}}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\Require{An integer $k>0$ and an odd integer $a<2^k$}
		\Ensure{$a^{-1} \pmod{2^k}$}
		%\Statex
		\Function{ModInverse2}{$a, 2^k$}
		\Let{$b_0$}{$1$}
		\For{$i = 0$ to $k-1$}
			\Let{$X_{i}$}{$b_{i} \pmod 2 $}
			\Let{$b_{i+1}$}{$\frac{b_{i}-aX_{i}}2$}
		\EndFor
		\State \Return{$x=(X_{k-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_2$}
		\EndFunction
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\section{An Algorithm of Inversion Mod $n^k$  for General $n$}
The main task of this section is to develop a method of computing modulo inverse with modulus being
a power of arbitrary integer bigger than $1$.

Let us fix integers $n>1, k>0$. Let $0<a<n^k$ be such that $\gcd(a,n) =1$, then $a$ has a unique radix-$n$ representation
\[
a = a_0+a_1n+a_2n^2+\cdots a_{k-1}n^{k-1},
\]
where $0\le a_i<n$.

Obviously $x=a^{-1} \pmod {n^k}$ exists. We write this number in a radix-$n$ form
\[
x = X_0+X_1n+X_2n^2+\cdots X_{k-1}n^{k-1},
\]
with $0\le X_i<n$. 



Let $c = a^{-1}\pmod n$, then $c=X_0$, and
\[
a_0X_0\equiv 1 \pmod n.
\]

We know that $ax = 1+n^k\ell$ for some integer $\ell \ge 0$. Putting the multiplication $ax$ in a manner as
schoolbook, we see that
\small{
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lllllllll}
	& & & $a_{k-1}$ & $a_{k-2}$ & $\cdots$ & $a_2$ & $a_1$ & $a_0$ \\
	& & & $X_{k-1}$ & $X_{k-2}$ & $\cdots$ & $X_2$ & $X_1$ & $X_0$ \\
	\hline
	& & & $a_{k-1}X_0$ & $a_{k-2}X_0$ & $\cdots$ & $a_2X_0$ & $a_1X_0$ & $a_0X_0$ \\
	& & $a_{k-1}X_1$ & $a_{k-2}X_1$  & $a_{k-3}X_1$ & $\cdots$ & $a_1X_1$ & $a_0X_1$& \\
	& $a_{k-1}X_2$ & $a_{k-2}X_2$ & $a_{k-3}X_2$ & $a_{k-4}X_2$ & $\cdots$  & $a_0X_2$& &\\
		&&&&&$\cdots$&&&\\
		&$a_3X_{k-2}$&$a_2X_{k-2}$&$a_1X_{k-2}$&$a_0X_{k-2}$&$\cdots$&&&\\
		&$a_2X_{k-1}$&$a_1X_{k-1}$&$a_0X_{k-1}$&&$\cdots$&&&\\
			&&&&&$\cdots$&&&\\
$\cdots$&$q_{k}$&$q_{k-1}$&$q_{k-2}$&$q_{k-3}$&$\cdots$&$q_1$&$q_0$&\\
\hline
&$*$& $*$ &  $0$ & $0$ &$\cdots$&$0$&$0$&$1$\\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
}\normalsize{}	
here $q_0, q_1, \cdots,q_{k-2}, q_{k-1}, \cdots$ are carries.
We observe that
\begin{enumerate}
\item The carries $q_0, q_1, \cdots,q_{k-3}, q_{k-2}$  satisfy
\begin{equation}\label{eq:carries}
\begin{array}{l}
	q_0 = \frac{a_0X_0-1}n\\
	q_1 = \frac{ q_0+a_1X_0+a_0X_1}n\\
		q_2 = \frac{ q_1+a_2X_0+a_1X_1+a_0X_2}n\\
\cdots \cdots\\
	q_{k-3} =\frac{q_{k-4}+a_{k-2}X_0+a_{k-3}X_1+\cdots+a_1X_{k-3}+a_0X_{k-2}}n\\
		q_{k-2} =\frac{q_{k-3}+a_{k-1}X_0+a_{k-2}X_1+\cdots+a_1X_{k-2}+a_0X_{k-1}}n.
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\item $X_0=a^{-1} \pmod n$ and the digits $X_1, \cdots, X_{k-2}, X_{k-1}$  can be solved out one by one through (\ref{eq:carries}). The
division by $n$ naturally appears in this process which corresponds to the right shifting operation. 
\end{enumerate}
From these observations, an algorithm for computing $a^{-1}\pmod {n^k}$ can be formulated as follows:
\begin{algorithm}
	\caption{Inversion Modulo a Power} 	\label{alg:XTY}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\Require{Integer $n>1$, integer $k>0$, integer $a<n^k, \ \gcd(n,a)=1$}
		\Ensure{$a^{-1} \pmod{n^k}$}
		%\Statex
\algstore{XTY}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\algrestore{XTY}	
		\Function{ModInverse}{$a, n^k$}
		\Let{$c$}{$a^{-1}\pmod n$}
		\Let{$T_0$}{$\frac{ca_0-1}n$}
		\Let{$X_0$}{$c$}
		\For{$i = 1$ to $k-1$}
		\Let{$T_{i}$}{$\frac{T_{i-1}+X_{i-1}a}n$}
		\Let{$X_{i}$}{$-cT_{i} \pmod n $}   %%%\Let{$X_{i}$}{$n-cT_{i} \pmod n $}	
	\EndFor
		\State \Return{$x=(X_{k-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_n$}
		\EndFunction
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


When $n=2$, the algorithm is simplified to algorithm \ref{alg:XTY-2}:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
	\caption{Inversion Modulo a Power of $2$
		\label{alg:XTY-2}}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\Require{An integer $k>0$ and an odd integer $a<2^k$}
		\Ensure{$a^{-1} \pmod{2^k}$}
		%\Statex
		\Function{ModInverse2}{$a, 2^k$}
		\Let{$T_0$}{$0$}
		\Let{$X_0$}{$1$}
		\For{$i = 1$ to $k-1$}
		\Let{$T_{i}$}{$\frac{T_{i-1}+X_{i-1}a}2$}
		\Let{$X_{i}$}{$T_{i} \pmod 2 $}
		\EndFor
		\State \Return{$x=(X_{k-1}\cdots X_1X_0)_2$}
		\EndFunction
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\

In the following, we have some further discussions on algorithm \ref{alg:XTY}.

Besides its generality for allowing modulus to be a power of any integer bigger than $1$,
Algorithm \ref{alg:XTY} also save one multiplication by a digit over 
Ko\c{c} algorithm (algorithm \ref{alg:Koc}). This is because the length of the {\bf for }
loop in our algorithm is $k-1$, instead of $k$. Each step in the loop has two such multiplications, 
but we have one multiplication in the initial step, this leaves one saving of one multiplication by a digit. 

However, a greater flexibility provided by our algorithm implies more optimization possibilities. As a particular example, we consider the case of $n=2^{64}$. We show that by utilizing the build-in arithmetic of a computer whose native bit width of a CPU's arithmetic instructions is $64$, a significant speed up is seen. The following table contains comparisons of our algorithm \ref{alg:XTY} for the setup of $n=64$ with (1) Ko\c{c} algorithm (algorithm \ref{alg:Koc-2}), and (2)
Hurchalla algorithm in \cite{Hur}, for $k=128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 3072$ and $4096$ (in our algorithm, these corresponding to $k=2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 48$ and $64$). 

The experiment are conducted on Unbuntu 24.04.2, with CPU AMD Ryzen 7 8845HS. Programs are compiled using gcc 14.2.0 with option {\tt -Ofast -march=native -flto}.
The numbers in the second, third and fourth columns are average running time in {\sl ns}.
\begin{center}
	\begin{tabular}{|p{2cm}|p{4cm}|p{4cm}|p{4cm}|}
		\hline 
		The length $k$ of modulus & algorithm \ref{alg:XTY} with $n=2^{64}$ and initial $a^{-1}\pmod n$ computed using Hurchalla alg. & Hurchalla algorithm in \cite{Hur} & Binary version of Ko\c{c} algorithm (algorithm \ref{alg:Koc-2})\\
\hline
