In this experiment, we use Hurchalla algorithm to compute $a^{-1}\pmod {2^{64}}$ for the initialization of $c\gets a^{-1}\pmod n$ in 
our algorithm, and all basic operations are done
by $64$-bit native machine arithmetic. This results a great gain of efficiency.
\section{Conclusions}
In this paper, we develop method of computing inverse modulo a power of arbitrary integer bigger than $1$. In formulation, our algorithm is comparable with a recent algorithm of Ko\c{c} for prime power, but with a motivation from the elementary schoolbook multiplication. Since the base (or radix) of our modulus is not restricted, we are able to compute $a^{-1}\pmod {n^k}$ for suitable $n>1$. The experiment using $n=2^{64}$ demonstrates a great performance over existing methods. The paper also describes an alternative proof of correctness of Ko\c{c} algorithm and explains how to use the proof to derive $a^{-1} \pmod {n^{s}}$ and $(n^s)^{-1}\pmod a$ for $s<k$ from the procedure of $a^{-1}\pmod {n^k}$.


