\begin{case}
 $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle = \langle G \rangle = \{l\}$    
\end{case}

In this case, we know that $R$ lies in one of the half plane defined by the line $l$, and let $R \subset l^{+}$. If the set $R_{C^{*}}$ also lies entirely on one side of the half plane defined by $l$, then the intersection of $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle$ and $\conv \langle R_{C^{*}} \rangle $ is empty and we are done by Theorem \ref{thm:main}. Hence, we assume the contrary that $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle$ and $\conv \langle R_{C^{*}} \rangle $ have a non-empty intersection. We observe that the points $ p \in R_{C^{*}}$ lie on half rays, such that $-p \in \partial C$. By our assumption, there should be at least two such half rays $t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$ such that $t_{1}$ lies in the half plane $l^{+}$, $t_{2}$ lies in the half plane $l^{-}$ and $\conv(t_{1},t_{2}) \cap l \neq \phi$. 

We show that we can choose $t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$ so that there are no other half rays $t \in R_{C^{*}}$ in between $t_{1}$ and $l$ and also in between $t_{2}$ and ${l}$. If such an additional ray $t$ exists , then either $-t \in \partial C$, in which 
case $t \in G = G_{C^{*}}$ and we get a contradiction to the fact that $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle = \langle G \rangle$ is spanned by a single line $l$. If $-t \not \in \partial C$, then $t \in R_{C^{*}}$ and we consider $t_{l} = t,  l \in \{1,2\}$. Consider the open cone in between the half ray $-t_{1}$ and $l$ as $S$. Since both $-t_{1}$ and $l$ lie on the boundary of $C$, the cone 
 $S$ is either in $C$ or in $C^{*}$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:boundary_one_dim}).
\begin{description}
    \item[Case (i) $S \subseteq C$.] 
    We note that as $S \subseteq l^{-}$, therefore $S$ is in the symmetric part of $C$ and $-S \subseteq C$. But the cone $-S$ is in between $l$ and the half ray $t_{1}$, where $t_{1} \in C^{*}$ and we get a contradiction since we obtain a set $-S$ in $C$ which contains points in $C^{*}$ on the half ray $t_{1}$ that are arbitrarily close to points in $C$ without having any points from the boundary of $C$ in between.
    \item[Case (ii) $S \subseteq C^{*}$.]
    In this case we realize that as $-t_{1} \in \partial C$, there is an adjacent open cone $S'$ to $S$, such that $S'$ is contained in between rays $-t_{1}$ and $-t_{3}, -t_{3} \in \partial C,  t_{3} \subseteq l^{-}$. As $ \langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle = \langle G \rangle $ contains a single line, therefore $t_{3} \in C^{*}$. Also, as $S' \subseteq l^{-}$ this implies that $-S' \subseteq C$ which contains points in $C^{*}$ on the half ray $t_{3}$ that are arbitrarily close to points in $C$ without having any points from the boundary of $C$ in between, which gives us a contradiction.
\end{description}
 \begin{case}
 $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle = \langle G \rangle = \{0\}$    
\end{case}

In this case, we know that $R$ lies entirely in a half plane defined by line $\pi$ and let $R \subseteq \pi^{+}$. All points on $\partial C$ lie on half rays $t$ such that $-t \subseteq C^{*}$.If the set $R_{C^{*}}$ also lies entirely on one side of the half plane defined by $\pi$, then the intersection of $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle$ and $\conv \langle R_{C^{*}} \rangle $ is empty and we are done by Theorem \ref{thm:main}. Hence, we assume the contrary that $\langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle$ and $\conv \langle R_{C^{*}} \rangle $ have a non-empty intersection. 

By our assumption, there should exist at least three half rays $t_{1}, t_{2}$ and $t_{3}$ such that not all of them lie in the same half plane with respect to $\pi$. Without loss of generality we choose the rays $t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$ such that $t_{1} \subseteq \pi^{+}$ and $t_{2} \subseteq \pi^{-}$ and there are no rays of $R_{C^{*}}$ in between $\pi, t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$. Then the open cone between the rays $-t_{1}$ and $-t_{2}$ contains the open cone between the line $\pi$ and $-t_{1}$ and we refer to this contained open cone as $S$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:boundary_zero_dim}).
\begin{description}
    \item[Case (i) $S \subseteq C$.] 
    We note that as $S \subseteq l^{-}$, therefore $S$ is in the symmetric part of $C$ and $-S \subseteq C$. But the cone $-S$ is in between $\pi$ and the half ray $t_{1}$, where $t_{1} \in C^{*}$ and we get a contradiction since we obtain a set $-S$ in $C$ which contains points in $C^{*}$ on the half ray $t_{1}$ that are arbitrarily close to points in $C$ without having any points from the boundary of $C$ in between.
    \item[Case (ii) $S \subseteq C^{*}$.]
    In this case we realize that as $-t_{1} \in \partial C$, there is an adjacent open cone $S'$ to $S$, such that $S'$ is contained in between rays $-t_{1}$ and $-t_{4}, -t_{4} \in \partial C,  t_{4} \subseteq l^{-}$. As $ \langle G_{C^{*}} \rangle = \langle G \rangle  = \{0\}$ , therefore $t_{4} \in C^{*}$. Also, as $S' \subseteq l^{-}$ this implies that $-S' \subseteq C$ which contains points in $C^{*}$ on the half ray $t_{3}$ that are arbitrarily close to points in $C$ without having any points from the boundary of $C$ in between, which gives us a contradiction.
\end{description}


\end{proof}

 \section{Examples}

\subsection{Examples in $\mathbb R^2$}

We describe certain examples of positivity sets in $\mathbb R^2$ here. One such class is the set of all \textit{convex polygons} in the plane. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:polygon__is_positivity_set}
Let $P \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{2}$ be a convex polygon (not necessarily bounded) such that $ \dim(P) =2$. Then $\text{int}(P)$ is a positivity set.   
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
We consider various cases for the convex polygon $P$.
\begin{case}
$P$ is unbounded.    
\end{case}
We choose four points $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}$ and $v_{4} \in \partial P$. Additionally, we choose two points $v_{5}$ and $v_{6}$ also on $\partial P$ such that the line segments $[v_{1}, v_{6}]$ and $[v_{4}, v_{5}]$ also lie on $\partial P$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:unb_conv_polygon_positivity_set}). Such a collection of six distinct points can always be obtained on the boundary of a unbounded convex polygon, unless it is a convex cone,  a case which is already covered previously. For $\text{int}(P)$ to be a positivity set, it implies there exists a hinge function $f$ such that it is positive in $\text{int(P)}$, zero on $\partial P$ and negative elsewhere. We now aim to construct one such hinge function and for that we fix four valleys $V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}$ and $V_{4}$ of the hinge function $f$ on the straight lines parallel to each other passing through the points $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}$ and $v_{4}$. The valleys $V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}$ and $V_{4}$ along with the boundary $\partial P$ divide the plane into 10 regions $R_{1}, \hdots, R_{10}$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:unb_conv_polygon_positivity_set}). Additionally, $f$ should also attain the value zero on all the points $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4}, v_{5}$ and $v_{6}$. Therefore, there exists a one dimensional subspace of hinge functions in the seven dimensional vector space of all hinge functions $f$ which have valleys/mountains on the lines given by the zero set of $V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}$ and $V_{4}$, given by the basis vectors $<1, x, y , V_{1}, V_{2}, V_{3}, V_{4}>$. 

