We observe that $f$ has the same sign within a single region, out of all the regions $R_{1}, \hdots, R_{10}$. Without loss of generality, we assume that $f$ attains positive values in the region $R_{1}$. As we move from the region $R_{1}$ to $R_{2}$, we do not encounter any point where $f$ attains the value zero, and as $f$ is continous, this implies that $f$ attains positive values also on the regions $R_{2}, R_{3}, R_{4}$ and $R_{5}$. Also, if we move from the region $R_{1}$ to $R_{10}$, $f$ attains the value of zero precisely on $\partial P$, therefore $f$ changes sign and hence is negative in the region $R_{10}$. Again, by using the continuity of $f$ we conclude that $f$ attains negative values on the regions $R_{6}, R_{7}, R_{8}$ and $R_{9}$. This provides us the desired hinge function as $f$.

\begin{case}
$P$ is bounded and not a triangle.    
\end{case}

We consider the convex polygon $P = \text{conv}(v_{1}, v_{2}, \hdots, v_{n}), n > 3$. As in the previous case, we construct a hinge function $f$ whose positivity set is $\text{int}(P)$. In order to do so we first consider a hinge function $g$ that has valleys along the line passing through the two vertices $v_{i}$ and $v_{j}$, where $v_{i} = v_{1}$ and $v_{j} \in \{v_{3}, v_{5}, \hdots, v_{n-1}, v_{n}\}$. Also, we choose another valley of $g$ to pass through the vertices $v_{2}$ and $v_{4}$ that are adjacent to $v_{1}$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:bound_conv_polygon_positivity_set}). Therefore, $g$ is a hinge function with $n-2$ valleys, and these valleys along with the boundary $\partial P$ divides the plane into finitely many regions. Additionally, $g$ also attains the value zero on all the points $v_{1}, v_{2}, \hdots , v_{n}$. Therefore, there exists a one dimensional subspace of hinge functions in the $n+1$ dimensional vector space of all hinge functions $f$ which have mountains/valleys amongst $V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}$ and $V_{n-2}$, given by the basis vectors $<1, x, y , V_{1}, V_{2}, \hdots, V_{n-2}>$. 

Again, using the continuity of $g$, we conclude that $g$ has the same sign for all points within one region. Without loss of generality we can assume that $g$ attains positive values in one of the regions bounded inside $P$, then as $g$ does not attain the value of zero for any point in $\text{int}(P)$, therefore we can conclude that $g$ attains positive values for all regions bounded within $P$. Similarly, we also conclude that for all regions in the complement of $P$ except the convex cone $C$, (cf. Figure \ref{fig:bound_conv_polygon_positivity_set}) $g$ attains negative values. \tbd{Could be elaborated in a better way later}

As $P$ is a convex polygon, therefore there exists a supporting hyperplane $H_{v{_1}}$ passing through $v_{1}$ such that $P$ lies in the half plane ${H_{v{_1}}}^{+}$ and the cone $C$ lies in the other half plane ${H_{v{_1}}}^{-}$. We now consider a hinge function $h : \mathbb{R}^{2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ which has a single valley along $H_{v{_1}}$ and is defined as 

\[
  h(x) =
  \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if $x \in {H_{v{_1}}}^{+}$} \\
    -N & \text{if $x \in {H_{v{_1}}}^{-}$}
  \end{cases}
\]

for some large positive value of $N \in \mathbb{R}$ such that $-N +g(x) < 0$ for all $x \in C$. Hence, we now obtain a hinge function $f = g+h$ whose positivity set is exactly $P$ and it is the desired hinge function.


\begin{case}
$P$ is a triangle.    
\end{case}

Consider $P = \text{conv} (v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3})$.  For $\text{int}(P)$ to be a positivity set we construct a hinge function $f$, which achieves positive values on $\text{int}(P)$. We specify three mountains $M_{1}, M_{2}$ and $M_{3}$ for $f$, where each $M_{i}$ passes through the vertex $v_{i}, i\in \{ 1, 2, 3\}$ and a point $\Tilde{v}_{j}$ on the line segment $[v_{1}, v_{2}]$, $j = \{1,2\}$. In order to compensate for the mountains appearing at the points $\Tilde{v}_{1}$ and $\Tilde{v}_{2}$, such that the function attains negative values outside of $\text{int}(P)$, there should be at least two valleys $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$ passing through $\Tilde{v}_{1}$ and $\Tilde{v}_{2}$ (cf. Figure \ref{fig:triangle_posit_set}). We choose two additional points $v_{4}$ and $v_{5}$ such that they lie on $\partial P$. 

Additionally, $f$ should also attain the value zero on the points $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4}, v_{5}, \Tilde{v}_{1}$ and $\Tilde{v}_{2}$. Therefore, there exists a one dimensional subspace of hinge functions in the eight dimensional vector space of all hinge functions $f$ which have valleys/mountains on the lines given by the zero set of $M_{1},M_{2},M_{3}, V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$, given by the basis vectors $<1, x, y , M_{1}, M_{2}, M_{3}, V_{1}, V_{2}>$. We observe that such an arrangement of valleys and mountains divides $\text{int}(P)$ into ten regions, and without loss of generality we can assume that $f$ attains positive values in one such region. As in the previous case we note that  the sign of the value of $f$
within a region remains the same and since there are no zeroes of $f$ within $\text{int}(P)$, we concldue that $f$ is positive in $\text{int}(P)$ and is the desired hinge function.
