\begin{lemma}
Let $P \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{2}$ be a convex polygon such that $ \dim(P) =2$. Then $(\partial P)^{C}$ is a positivity set.   
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $P = \conv(V) = \conv(v_{!}, v_{2}, \hdots, v_{n}), n \geq 3$. Then $\partial P$ is a graph, such that $\partial P = G_{P} = (V, E)$, where $E$ is the collection of all 1-dimensional faces of $P$ where each $e \in E$ is of the form $e = \conv(v_{i}, v_{j})$ for some $i,j \in \{1,2,\hdots n\}$. Since a convex polygon is the intersection of a finitely many halfplanes, therefore for each edge $e \in E$, we obtain a supporting hyperplane $H_{e}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{2}$. Hence, the convex polygon $P$ describes a hyperplane (line) arrangement in $\mathbb{R}^{2}$

\[ \mathcal{H}_{P} = \{H_{e} | e \in E \} \]

For each line $H_{e}$ in $\mathcal{H}_{P}$, we refer to the half plane with non-empty intersection with $P$ as ${H_{e}}^{+}$ and the other half plane as ${H_{e}}^{+}$. For each $e \in E$, we define a hinge function $f_{e}$,

\[ f_{e}(x)  =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{for }  x \in {H_{e}}^{+}\\
c>0 & \text{for }  x \in {H_{e}}^{-}\\
\end{cases}  \]

Then for the hinge function $f = \sum_{e \in E} f_{e}$, we note that $f$ attains positive values in $P^{C}$ and attains the value of zero in $\text{int}(P)$ and $\partial P$. We also know by Theorem \ref{thm:polygon__is_positivity_set} that there exists a hinge function $g$ such that $\text{int}(P)$ is a positivity set. We consider the hinge function 

\[ h  = f + \epsilon \cdot g \]

for some $\epsilon > 0$. Then for a choice of $\epsilon$ which is arbitrarily small, such that 

\[ f (x) +  \epsilon \cdot g(x) > 0, \quad \text{for } x \in \text{int(P)} \cup P^{C}  \]

we see that the positivity set of $h$ is exactly $(\partial P)^{C}$.
\end{proof}

