In reasoning about probabilities in AI / ML, what usually plays the key role is not a \emph{small} joint probability distribution model, but a model that can be efficiently encoded as a \emph{Bayesian network} (BN).

A BN $\cB=(\cG,P)$ consists of a DAG $\cG$ over nodes representing random variables $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ and $P$ which factorizes over $\cG$, meaning that hat the joint probability $\compactEquals{P(x_1,\myldots,x_n)}=\prod_{i=1}^n  \compactEquals{P(x_i\mid \mbox{\it pa}_i)}$, where $\mbox{\it pa}_i$ denotes the values $x_{j_1},\ldots,x_{j_k}$ of parents  $\mbox{\it Pa}_i$ of $X_i$ in $\cG$. Then the distribution $P$ is specified as a set of conditional probability tables for each 
variable $X_i$ conditioning on its parents in $\cG$ \cite{koller2009probabilistic}. So, for example, in the case of binary variables, even if the array representing the joint distribution has $2^n$ non-zero elements, it can still be compactly represented by a set of arrays of total size $\sum_{i=1}^n 2^{1+|\mbox{\it \footnotesize Pa}_{i}|}$. This representation has many additional advantages, for example enabling efficient probabilistic inference.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\text{SAT}_{sm}$ problem: for an input, a formula $\varphi$, decide whether there exists a satisfying small model (encoding a probability distribution) for $\varphi$.

\item MC problem in small models: for an input, a formula $\varphi$ and a (small) model $P$, decide whether $\varphi$ is true in $P$. (todo: Applications, motivations)

\item $\text{SAT}_{DAG}$ problem: for an input, a formula $\varphi$ and a DAG $\cG$, decide whether there exists a satisfying BN model $(\cG,P)$ for $\varphi$.

\item MC problem in Bayesian network models: for an input, a formula $\varphi$ and a BN $\cB=(\cG,P)$, decide whether $\varphi$ is true in $\cB$.
(This problem is particularly important in AI / ML.)

