\begin{abstract}
Federated learning(FL) is used for distributed scenarios to train artificial intelligence(AI) models while ensuring users' privacy. In federated learning scenario, the server generally never knows about users' data. This type of concept makes the AI training process efficient in terms of data privacy. However, regarding model performance, federated AI models may not sufficiently satisfy AI users' expectations. Furthermore, AI users have a wide range of different needs. It is not easy to satisfy the whole users needs. These types of issues can be addressed through AI model optimization, fine-tuning, or personalization to achieve optimal model performance. To address model optimization challenges, we propose reference model-based federated learning for optimal fine-tuning, which overcomes catastrophic forgetting in each round. This method is derived from Bayesian parameter-efficient transfer learning, which includes an optimal proximal term and enables overcoming the catastrophic forgetting issue in each round by utilizing a reference model that incorporates previous model parameters. As a result, this method achieves both high model performance and low computing cost.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Privacy Preserving Machine Learning, Federated Learning, Bayesian Optimization, Transfer Learning, Fine-Tune.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Recently, in the AI industry, federated learning (FL) has been proposed as a prominent solution to protect users' data privacy while allowing collaborative model training between independent affiliations. Users' data are fundamentally protected because clients share only updated local model parameters (resulting from local training using their local data) and other non-private values with the server or other clients. The server then computes the global model by aggregating the local models received by the clients. However, as highlighted in \cite{fedavg}, FL solutions face numerous challenges. Our focus is on optimizing model performance and decreasing computing costs on client devices.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Model performance optimization in FL scenarios.
    \item Decreasing computing cost and energy consumption. 
    \item How to protect the model from malicious users.
\end{enumerate} 

In the related work section, we introduce existing approaches (FedAvg, FedProx, FedOpt, Bayesian Transfer Learning) for model optimization in FL scenarios, along with their limitations regarding client computing cost and catastrophic forgetting \cite{mccloskey1989catastrophic}\cite{french1999catastrophic} \cite{goodfellow2013empirical} in each round. We then introduce our FedRef: a communication-efficient Bayesian fine-tuning approach with a reference model. This concept overcomes catastrophic forgetting in each round by integrating previous model features into the maximum a posteriori (MAP) problem. 

In existing work, the MAP problem was applied as an objective function for optimizing model transfer learning between a target model with pre-trained parameters and a source model. On the other hand, traditional model optimization in FL scenarios used a proximal term to minimize deviation from the global model. However, the proximal term can be denoted as a MAP problem. In this paper, we define the MAP problem as an objective function that is updated with aggregated models from previous rounds only on the server-side, thereby minimizing clients' computing cost.

In our experiments, we applied FedRef on MNIST, CIFAR10, FeTs2022 data partitioning from distributed condition for non-IID(Independent and Identically Distributed) setting. We defined each baseline and then analyzed model performances, as baseline models differ for each dataset. Especially, on the FeTs2022, the data is derived in medical imaging area for brain tumor segmentation. In this case, the client-side can be a hospital, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:federatedlearningsystem}.   
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.40\textwidth]{res/fig/FL.png}
    \caption{Basic federated learning system.}
    \label{fig:federatedlearningsystem}
\end{figure}
As a result, our FedRef achieves high model performance, despite minimizing clients' computing cost.



\section{Related Work}
\subsubsection{FedAvg}
Communication-efficient learning of deep networks from decentralized data \cite{fedavg}, they proposed typical system of federated learning concept. The paper, which introduced FedAvg, demonstrated higher model performance than FedSGD on the MNIST classification task under both IID and non-IID conditions using the Federated Averaging method. Federated Averaging is based on the stochastic gradient descent (SGD) concept \cite{chen2016revisiting}, which aggregates client models weighted by the number of samples from each client, as shown in equation (\ref{averaging}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{averaging} \theta_{r+1} = \sum_{i=1}^K \frac{n_i}{n} \theta_{i,r}
\end{equation}
$r$: round of repeating server routine, $\theta_{i}$ : $i$ index client's model parameter, $n_i$: $i$ index client's number of samples, $n$: total number of clients' samples. We also selected this type of aggregation, but it is easy to apply other weighted aggregation concepts to our FedRef solution. On the client side, local training is represented by equation (\ref{localtraining}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{localtraining} \theta_{i}^{(s+1)} = \theta_{i}^{(s)} - \eta \nabla F_i(\theta_{i}^{(s)}; \mathcal{B}_i^{(s)})
\end{equation}
$s$ : number of local steps for local training, $\eta$: learning rate, $\mathcal{B}_i^{(s)}$: mini-batch sampled from $i$ index client local dataset on step $s$, $F_i(\theta_{i}^{(s)}; \mathcal{B}_i^{(s)})$: local loss function evaluated on a mini-batch. The local training in our FedRef client update is based on the SGD solution. In algorithm \ref{alg:fedavg}, it show whole FedAvg process flow.
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{FedAvg: Communication-efficient learning of deep networks from decentralized data\label{alg:fedavg}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Initialize} global model weights $\theta_0$
\For{each round $r = 1, 2, \dots, R$}
    \State Server selects of clients $\mathcal{S}_r \subseteq \{1, \dots, K\}$
    \ForAll{client $k \in \mathcal{S}_r$ \textbf{in parallel}}
        \State Client $k$ receives $\theta_r$ from the server
        \State Client $k$ performs local training:
        \State \quad $\theta_{r+1}^k \leftarrow \text{ClientUpdate}(k, 
        \theta_r)$
    \EndFor
    \State Server aggregates the client updates:
    \State \quad $\theta_{r+1} \leftarrow \sum_{k \in \mathcal{S}_r} \frac{n_k}{n_{\mathcal{S}_r}} \theta_{r+1}^k$
    \State \quad $n_{\mathcal{S}_r} = \sum_{k \in \mathcal{S}_r} n_k$
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\subsubsection{FedProx}  
Federated Optimization in Heterogeneous Networks \cite{fedprox}, they proposed equation (\ref{equation1}) named "Proximal Term" to objective function for ensuring that local models do not deviate significantly from the global model. This concept enables optimizing the model to account for data skew and heterogeneity. However, it does not consider clients' computing costs, as clients must compute the proximal term and send it to the server.

\begin{equation}
    \label{equation1} \min_{\theta_k} \left(\mathbf{E}_{\mathcal{D}_k} \left[ \mathcal{L}(\theta_k, \mathcal{D}_k) \right] + \frac{\mu}{2} \| \theta_k - \theta_{\text{global}} \|^2 \right)
\end{equation}
$k$ : a client index, $\theta_k$ : a client $k$ local model parameter, $\mathcal{L}(\theta_k, \mathcal{D}_k)$ : client $k$ local data $\mathcal{D}_k$ cost function, $\theta_{\text{global}}$ : global model parameter, $\mu$: hyperparameter for proximal term strength, $\| \theta_k - \theta_{\text{global}} \|^2$: L2(Euclidean) distance between local model parameter and global model parameter. The complete FedProx Algorithm \ref{alg:fedprox} is shown below.
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{FedProx: Federated Optimization in Heterogeneous Networks \label{alg:fedprox}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Initialize} global model weights $\theta_0$
\For{each round $r = 1, 2, \dots, R$}
    \State Server selects clients $\mathcal{S}_r \subseteq \{1, \dots, K\}$
    \ForAll{client $k \in \mathcal{S}_r$ \textbf{in parallel}}
        \State Client $k$ receives $\theta_r$ from the server
        \State Client $k$ performs local training:
        \State \quad $\theta_{r+1}^k \leftarrow \text{ClientUpdate\_Prox}(k, \theta_r, \mu)$
    \EndFor
    \State Server aggregates the client updates:
    \State \quad $\theta_{r+1} \leftarrow \sum_{k \in \mathcal{S}_r} \frac{n_k}{n_{\mathcal{S}_r}} \theta_{r+1}^k$
    \State \quad where $n_k$ is the number of samples at client $k$, and $n_{\mathcal{S}_r} = \sum_{k \in \mathcal{S}_r} n_k$
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
In the server side, they just aggregate and return model parameter to clients. This led to the issue of concentrated computing costs on the client. Therefore, on the client side, Algorithm \ref{alg:clientprox} introduces local training with a proximal term, which increases the client computing cost.
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{ClientUpdate\_Prox($k$, $\theta_r$, $\mu$) \label{alg:clientprox}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Initialize local model $\theta \leftarrow \theta_r$
\For{each local epoch $e = 1, 2, \dots, E$}
    \For{each minibatch $b$ in local data $\mathcal{D}_k$}
        \State Compute gradient:
        \State \quad $g \leftarrow \nabla f_k(\theta; b) + \mu(\theta - \theta_r)$
        \State Update local model:
        \State \quad $\theta \leftarrow \theta - \eta g$
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return $\theta$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{FedOpt}
Adaptive Federated Optimization \cite{reddi2021adaptivefederatedoptimization}, they introduced and included optimization concept merged with (FedADAGRAD, FedADAM, FedYOGI). They included each different specific proximal terms. Following FedADAGRAD equation (\ref{fedadagrad}):
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
     g_r = \frac{1}{K} \sum_{k=1}^{K} \Delta \theta_r^{(k)}, \nonumber\\
     G_r = G_{r-1} + g_r^2, \nonumber \\
     \theta_{r+1} = \theta_r - \eta \cdot \frac{g_r}{\sqrt{G_r} + \tau} \label{fedadagrad}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
$g_r$: averaged update from all participating clients, $G_r$: accumulated sum of squared gradients (maintained on the server), $\tau$: small constant for numerical stability (e.g., $10^{-6}$).
They introduced optimization of local models to a variant that considers global data distribution with two types of different parameters. Following FedADAM equation (\ref{fedadam}):
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
    \label{fedadam}
    m_r = \beta_1 m_{r-1} + (1 - \beta_1) g_r, 
    \nonumber\\ v_r = \beta_2 v_{r-1} + (1 - \beta_2) g_r^2,
    \nonumber\\ \hat{m}_r = \frac{m_r}{1 - \beta_1^r}, \, \hat{v}_r = \frac{v_r}{1 - \beta_2^r}, \nonumber \\ \theta_{r+1} = \theta_r - \eta \cdot \frac{\hat{m}_r}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_r} + \tau}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
$m_r$: first moment vector (mean of gradients), $v_r$: second moment vector (uncentered variance of gradients), $\hat{m}_r, \hat{v}_r$: bias-corrected moment estimates, $\beta_1, \beta_2$: decay rates for first and second moments (e.g., $\beta_1 = 0.9, \beta_2 = 0.999$). The key term in FedADAM also denotes the variance between aggregated gradients and uncentered model parameters. Finally following FedYOGI equation (\ref{fedyogi}):

\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
    \label{fedyogi}
    v_t = v_{r-1} - (1 - \beta_2) \cdot sign(v_{r-1} - g_r^2) \cdot g_r^2,
    \nonumber\\ m_r = \beta_1 m_{r-1} + (1 - \beta_1) g_r,
    \nonumber\\ \hat{m}_r = \frac{m_r}{1 - \beta_1^r} \, \hat{v}_r = \frac{v_r}{1 - \beta_2^r},
    \nonumber\\ w_{r+1} = w_r - \eta \cdot \frac{\hat{m}_r}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_r} + \tau}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
sign$(\cdot)$: Element-wise sign function. This paper introduced various proximal term concepts (FedADAGRAD, FedADAM, FedYOGI) that are automatically selected based on specific conditions. For details, we present the complete FedOpt in Algorithm\ref{alg:fedopt}:
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{FedOpt: Adaptive Federated Optimization \label{alg:fedopt}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Input: $\theta_0$, \texttt{CLIENTOPT}, \texttt{SERVEROPT} \\
\For{$r = 0, \dots, R-1$}
    \State Sample a subset $\mathcal{S}$ of clients
    \State $\theta_{r,0}^{i} = \theta_r$
    \For{each client $i \in \mathcal{S}$ \textbf{in parallel}}
        \For{$k = 0, \dots, K - 1$}
            \State Compute $g_{r,k}^{i}$ of $\nabla F_i(\theta_{r,k}^{i})$
            \State $\theta_{r,k+1}^{i} = \texttt{CLIENTOPT}(\theta_{r,k}^{i}, g_{r,k}^{i}, \eta_l, r)$
        \EndFor
        \State $\Delta_r^i = \theta_{r,K}^{i} - \theta_r$
    \EndFor
    \State $\Delta_r = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{S}} \Delta_r^i$
    \State $\theta_{r+1} = \texttt{SERVEROPT}(\theta_r, -\Delta_r, \eta, r)$
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
These methods also required client-side computing for the proximal term, which needed to be optimized. Regarding state-of-the-art strategies, recent similar research (FedDyn \cite{jin2023feddyn}) has been proposed, which adaptively selects the optimal strategy. However, both FedOpt and FedDyn needed client side computing costs for specific proximal terms. 
\subsubsection{Bayesian Transfer Learning}
Bayesian parameter-efficient fine-tuning for overcoming catastrophic forgetting \cite{bayesian}, This paper proposed a Bayesian transfer learning concept for overcoming the catastrophic forgetting issue and formulated the optimal model fine-tuning cost as a Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) problem in equation(\ref{eq:map}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:map} \theta^{*} = \arg\max_{\theta} \left[ \log p(D_B|\theta) + \log p(\theta|D_A) \right]
\end{equation}
$D_A$: data used for pretraining, $D_B$: data used for downstream fine-tuning.
In equation (\ref{eq:map}), $\log p(\theta|D_A)$ can be approximated by Laplace Approximation \cite{mackay1992practical} to yield equation (\ref{eq:laplace}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:laplace}\log p(\theta|D_A) \approx f(\theta_0) + \tfrac12 (\theta - \theta_0)^\top H (\theta - \theta_0)
\end{equation}
$H$: negative Hessian of the log-posterior, approximated as the FIM(Fisher Information Matrix).
Finally, they defined the objective function for the optimization of transfer learning in equation (\ref{eq:objectf}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:objectf} \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \underbrace{L_B(\theta)}_{\text{task loss}} + \lambda\, (\theta - \theta_0)^\top F\, (\theta - \theta_0)
\end{equation}
$\lambda$: regularization strength, $F$: approximation of the Fisher Information Matrix or Hessian. According to this paper, to decrease computing cost and simplify the FIM matrix to an identity matrix, despite decreasing the capability of detecting model attention (which has key points affecting model performance), equation (\ref{eq:objectf}) can be simplified to equation (\ref{eq:l2sp}).
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:l2sp} 
\mathcal{L}_{\text{l2-sp}}(\theta) = L_B(\theta) + \lambda \sum_{k} (\theta_k - \theta_{0,k})^2
\end{equation}
Despite being simplified, equation (\ref{eq:l2sp}) has been shown to increase the rate of preventing catastrophic forgetting in transfer learning \cite{xuhong2018explicit}\cite{kirkpatrick2017overcoming}. For other Hessian metric assumptions, they described Kronecker-factored approximation\cite{martens2015optimizing}\cite{george2018fast}, which also considers the covariance between parameters within each layer.
As a result, this paper introduced optimization of transfer learning between only two models: one a pre-trained model intended for transfer, and the other a target model. However, in an FL scenario, the server must aggregate and optimize many different client models. Therefore, we need a definition of a new MAP cost problem for FL scenarios.

\section{FedRef: Communication-Efficient Bayesian Fine
Tuning with Reference Model}
For optimal model performance and low client computing cost, we proposed FedRef: communication-efficient Bayesian fine tuning with reference model which overcome catastrophic forgetting by inferring of previous rounds model. We carefully defined the model proximal term as a MAP problem for FL scenarios in equation (\ref{eq:flmap}) derived from the Bayesian fine-tuning paper\cite{bayesian}.
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
     \theta^{*} = \arg\max_{\theta} [\log p( \theta|D_{ref})+
     \log p(D_1|\theta)+\nonumber\\ log p(D_2|\theta)+\dots + \log p(D_K|\theta) ] \label{eq:flmap}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
Selected client numbers $k \in [1,2,3,..,K]$, where $K$ is the total number of selected clients. $D_{ref}$: represents synthetic data of a reference model that incorporates features from previous rounds. $D_{ref}$ is defined solely for explaining our optimal MAP problem (\ref{eq:objectref}). This concept enables overcoming catastrophic forgetting in each round by integrating previous round features into the MAP problem and then optimizing the integrated MAP value. Finally, the objective function derived in (\ref{eq:flmap}) can be expressed by combining equations (\ref{eq:laplace}, \ref{eq:objectf}, \ref{eq:l2sp}) to form equation (\ref{eq:objectref}).
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
    \label{eq:objectref} L_{ref}(\theta^1,\theta^2, \forall F)= \sum_{k}^{K} F_k \cdot \mathrm{diag}(W_1, \dots, W_K) \nonumber \\
        + \lambda \sum_{i} (\theta^1_i - \theta^1_{0,i})^2
        + \lambda \sum_{i} (\theta^2_i - \theta^2_{0,i})^2
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
In equation (\ref{eq:objectref}), the constant value $\sum_{k}^{K} F_k$ denotes the sum of client losses. The diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(W_1, \dots, W_K)$ represents aggregation weights (e.g. $\frac{n_i}{n}$). $\sum_{i} (\theta_i - \theta_{0,i})^2$ signifies $L_2$ regularization of model $\theta-\theta_0$. Regarding parameter requirements, only clients' losses are needed for our FedRef concept. Only on the server side, model optimization is performed, which can decrease both client computing cost and communication cost. In the FedRef, $\theta^2$ should be set as the reference model $\theta_{ref}$. For detail on $\theta_{ref}$, we defined the reference model as shown in equation \ref{eq:refmodel}.
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:refmodel} \theta_{r+1}^2 = \text{A}(\theta^1_{r-p}, \theta^1_{r-p+1}, \dots, \theta^1_r)
\end{equation}
In the equation (\ref{eq:refmodel}), $p$: the number of selected subset of previous aggregation model which can be set heuristically and $\text{A}$ function should be as following a equation \ref{eq:aggreation}.
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:aggreation} \text{A}(\forall\theta ) =
     \sum_{i=1}^p \frac{1}{p} \theta^1_{r+1-i}
\end{equation}
Experimentally, a suitable value for $p$ was analyzed to be 5.
For the whole algorithm, we denoted FedRef at following algorithm\ref{alg:fedref}:

\begin{algorithm}[]
\caption{FedRef: Communication-Efficient Bayesian Fine Tuning with Reference Model}\label{alg:fedref}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Client:}
\For {epoch} 
    \For {batch}
\\ \quad \quad \quad
            $\theta_{i}^{(s+1)} = \theta_{i}^{(s)} - \eta \nabla F_i(\theta_{i}^{(s)}; \mathcal{B}_i^{(s)})$
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\\
send $\theta$ , $F(\theta^{(s)}; \mathcal{B}^{(s)})$ to Server\\
\State \textbf{Server:}
\For {round}
  \\ \quad $K$-client select 
  \\ \quad $A_r$ $\leftarrow$ $\text{Aggregation}(\theta_1, \theta_2, \theta_3...\theta_K)$ 
  \\ \quad $R_r$ $\leftarrow$ $\text{Aggregation}(A_{r-p}...,A_{r-1},A_r)$ 
  \\ \quad $\theta$ $\leftarrow$ $\text{Bayesian Optimization}(A_r, R_r, \forall F(\theta^{(s)}; \mathcal{B}^{(s)}))$
  \\ \quad broadcast $\theta$ to Clients
\EndFor 
\\
\State \textbf{Aggregation($\forall \theta)$:}
\\ \quad $\theta_{r+1} = \sum_{i=1}^K \frac{n_i}{n} \theta_{i,r+1}$
\\return $\theta$
\\
\State \textbf{Bayesian Optimization($\theta^1, \theta^2$):}
  \\ \quad  $\theta^1 \leftarrow \theta^1-\eta\times\nabla{L}_{ref}(\theta^1,\theta^2)$
 \\ return $\theta^1$\\

% }
\State \textbf{Output} \,$\theta$
\end{algorithmic}\end{algorithm}

\section{Experiment}
\subsection{Experiment Setting}
\subsubsection{Global}
We developed a federated learning system using the Flower framework \cite{beutel2020flower}. For detailed experimental conditions, table~\ref{tab1} presents the global computing environment settings.

\begin{table}[hbt!]\begin{center}
\caption{Experiment Environment}\label{tab1}
