\hline
Setting &  Specification  \\
\hline
FL Framework & Flower: a friendly federated learning framework  \\
Language &  Python \\
Operation System & Linux 24.04 LTS \\
GPU & Nvidia RTX 4090 \\
Tools & Visual studio code\\
\hline
\end{tabular}\end{center}
\end{table}
Within the Python language environment, the Flower framework enables easy construction of federated learning servers and clients from high-level code, based on Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) and Functional Programming (FP), for privacy-preserving machine learning strategies(e.g. FedAvg, FedProx, FedOpt, etc.).
\subsubsection{MNIST}
The MNIST (Modified National Institute of Standards and Technology) dataset\cite{lecun-mnist-2010} is one of the most widely used datasets in the field of machine learning and computer vision. It serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of various algorithms, particularly in the area of handwritten digit recognition.
We discuss federated leaning models that have been applied to this dataset.
Each client has a partitioning rate of $\text{[50 to 90\%]}$ for train samples, and this rate is randomly varied for the non-IID setting. For the model baseline, MobileNet  \cite{howard2019searching} is applied for our MNIST classification task experiment.  
\subsubsection{CIFAR10}
The CIFAR-10 (Canadian Institute for Advanced Research) dataset\cite{Krizhevsky09learningmultiple} is one of the most prominent and widely used image datasets in machine learning and computer vision research. It consists of 60,000 32x32 color images across 10 distinct classes, making it a popular benchmark for evaluating the performance of classification algorithms, particularly in the realm of image recognition. The data partitioning mechanism for this dataset is the same as the MNIST setting. For the model baseline, ResNet \cite{he2016deep} is applied for CIFAR10 classification task.
\subsubsection{FeTs2022}
The FeTs2022 dataset refers to the dataset used in the Federated Tumor Segmentation (FeTS) Challenge 2022, which focused on brain tumor segmentation using federated learning approaches. The data includes multimodal brain MRI scans of glioma patients. Each patient folder typically has the four MRI modalities (T1-weighted, T1-weighted post-contrast, T2-weighted, T2 Fluid Attenuated Inversion Recovery). The data labels include segmentations of three tumor sub-regions (Enhancing tumor, Tumor core, Whole tumor). The data can be downloaded at official Synapse site \cite{fets2022}. We perform the segmentation task for evaluating FedRef on FeTs2022 data which have specific non-IID data partitioning at Fig. \ref{fig:partitioning}.
