    Using Lemma~\ref{lem_preprocessing_d_to_1}, let $\tilde\X$ be an unweighted instance of $d$-to-$1$ games such that  $\qval_{2k}(\tilde\X)=1$, $\val(\tilde \X)<\frac{\epsilon}{t^2}$, $\deg_\X(x)$ is constant over all $x\in X_A$, and
    $\pi_{x_Ax_B}$ is exact $d$-to-$1$ for each edge $\{x_A,x_B\}\in\Eset(\X)$. 
    %
    We consider the reduction from~\cite{Dinur09:sicomp}, that constructs an instance $\X$ of $d$-to-$d$ games as follows. Recall that $\tilde\X$ is a bipartite graph on bipartition $X_A,X_B$, and let $Y_A$, $Y_B$ be the alphabet of the corresponding $d$-to-$1$ game. The vertex set $X$ of $\X$ is $X_A$, and the alphabet $Y$ of the $d$-to-$d$ game is $Y_A$. For each pair $\{x_A,x_B\}$, $\{x_A',x_B'\}$ of edges of $\tilde\X$, we add one edge $\{x_A,x_A'\}$ in $\X$ if and only if $x_B=x_B'$. The corresponding constraint is
    \begin{align*}
        \psi(x_A,x_A')
        =
        \{(y_A,y_A')\in Y_A^2\mbox{ s.t. } \pi_{x_Ax_B}(y_A)=\pi_{x_A'x_B'}(y_A')\}.
    \end{align*}
    It is easy to check that the constraint defined above is $d$-to-$d$, so $\X$ is an instance of $d$-to-$d$ game.
    %
    On the soundness side, it was shown in~\cite[Lemma~A.9]{Dinur09:sicomp} that $\val(\tilde \X)<\frac{\epsilon}{t^2}$ implies $\ival_t(\X)<\epsilon$. We shall prove that $\qval_k(\X)=1$. 

    Take a $2k$-compatible quantum strategy $\mathfrak{E}$ over a Hilbert space $\HH$, for $\tilde\X$ witnessing the fact that $\qval_{2k}(\tilde\X)=1$. We define $F_{x,y}=E_{x,y}$ for each $x\in X=X_A$ and each $y\in Y=Y_A$. Note that each $F_{x,y}$ is a projector on $\HH$. Moreover,
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{y\in Y}F_{x,y}
        =
        \sum_{y\in Y}E_{x,y}
        =
        \id_\HH,
    \end{align*}
    and $F_{x,y}F_{x,y'}=E_{x,y}E_{x,y'}=O$ whenever $y\neq y'$. Hence, the set $\mathfrak{F_{x,y}\mbox{ s.t. }x\in X,y\in Y}$ is a quantum strategy for $\X$. 

    Take an edge $\{x,x'\}$ of $\X$ and a pair $(y,y')\in Y^2\setminus \psi(x,x')$. By construction of $\X$, there exists some $x_B\in X_B$ such that $\{x,x_B\}$ and $\{x',x_B\}$ are both edges of $\tilde\X$. On the other hand, since $(y,y')\not\in\psi(x,x')$, we have $\pi_{xx_B}(y)\neq\pi_{x'x_B}(y')$. As a consequence, since the quantum strategy $\mathfrak{E}$ is perfect, for each $y_B\in Y_B$ it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
        E_{x,y}E_{x_By_B}\neq O
        \quad\Rightarrow \quad
        y_B=\pi_{xx_B}(y)\neq\pi_{x'x_B}(y')
        \quad\Rightarrow \quad
        E_{x',y'}E_{x_By_B}= O,
    \end{align*}
    which means that 
    \begin{align*}
        E_{x,y}E_{x_By_B}E_{x',y'}E_{x_By_B}=O.
    \end{align*}
    Since that the vertices $x'$ and $x_B$ are adjacent in $\tilde \X$, the corresponding projectors commute, so we obtain
    \begin{align*}
        E_{x,y}E_{x',y'}E_{x_By_B}=O
    \end{align*}
    for each $y_B\in Y_B$. Summing over $Y_B$ yields
    \begin{align*}
        O=\sum_{y_B\in Y_B}E_{x,y}E_{x',y'}E_{x_By_B}
        =
        E_{x,y}E_{x',y'}\sum_{y_B\in Y_B}E_{x_By_B}
        =
        E_{x,y}E_{x',y'}\id_\HH
        =
        E_{x,y}E_{x',y'}
        =
        F_{x,y}F_{x',y'},
    \end{align*}
    whence we deduce that the quantum strategy $\mathfrak{F}$ is perfect.
%
Finally, it is clear from the definition of $\X$ that $\dist_\X(x,x')=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\dist_{\tilde\X}(x,x')$ for each pair of vertices $x,x'\in X$. Hence, the $k$-compatibility of $\mathfrak{F}$ follows directly from the $2k$-compatibility of $\mathfrak{E}$.
\end{proof}

