\begin{abstract}
Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) has transformed daily tasks from video transcription to workplace hiring. ASR systems' growing use warrants robust and standardized auditing approaches to ensure automated transcriptions of high and equitable quality. This is especially critical for people with speech and language disorders (such as aphasia) who may disproportionately depend on ASR systems to navigate everyday life. In this work, we identify three pitfalls in existing standard ASR auditing procedures, and demonstrate how addressing them impacts audit results via a case study of six popular ASR systems' performance for aphasia speakers. First, audits often adhere to a single method of text standardization during data pre-processing, which (a) masks variability in ASR performance from applying different standardization methods, and (b) may not be consistent with how users---especially those from marginalized speech communities---would want their transcriptions to be standardized. Second, audits often display high-level demographic findings without further considering performance disparities among (a) more nuanced demographic subgroups, and (b) relevant covariates capturing acoustic information from the input audio. Third, audits often rely on a single gold-standard metric---the Word Error Rate---which does not fully capture the extent of errors arising from generative AI models, such as transcription hallucinations. We propose a more holistic auditing framework that accounts for these three pitfalls, and exemplify its results in our case study, finding consistently worse ASR performance for aphasia speakers relative to a control group. We call on practitioners to implement these robust ASR auditing practices that remain flexible to the rapidly changing ASR landscape.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) technologies transcribe spoken audio into written text and are increasingly integrated into daily life, from powering virtual assistants to generating meeting transcriptions. In high-stakes settings like healthcare, job interviews, and court reporting, inaccuracies in ASR outputs can cause direct harm, particularly to marginalized speakers---ranging from African American English speakers \cite{koenecke2020racial}, to people who stutter \cite{lea2023user}, to Arabic-speaking refugees \cite{bircan2024machine}. As such, it is critical to regularly examine the performance of state-of-the-art ASR services and investigate the extent of ASR-based biases across user populations. Existing academic audit studies have shed light on the dangers of ASR technology, finding different levels of accessibility and performance for marginalized demographic populations, including by race \cite{koenecke2020racial, martin2023bias, mengesha2021don}, gender \cite{feng2021quantifying, feng2024towards, attanasio2024multilingual}, and medical condition \cite{moore2018whistle, lea2023user, HidalgoLopez2023, Zhao2024}. Increasingly, ASR service providers themselves are self-auditing the ASR landscape, reporting better performance of their product relative to competitors~\cite{assemblyai2024,hughes2023ursa,radford2023robust,deepgram2024}.

In the standard audit, a nominal value (ground truth) is compared to an actual value (ASR output)~\cite{Sloane2023}, but this procedure often overlooks nuances and assumptions related to specific user communities, risking audit results that lack robustness or fail to account for marginalized groups' lived experiences \cite{barocas2021designing}. To date, most ASR audits often follow a relatively standard procedure~\cite{radford2023robust,lea2023user,koenecke2020racial}. First, transcribed text is ``cleaned'' in a standardized way, removing capitalization, punctuation, dysfluencies, etc. Next, this cleaned text is compared to a similarly-cleaned ``gold standard'' ground truth---i.e., a transcription of what was truly said---and ASR performance is quantified by a single error rate metric. Finally, ASR performance may be compared between a control group and a potentially harmed population. However, we argue that each of these steps in the pipeline must be updated to improve standard auditing procedures for ASR models---especially given advances in generative AI, and the possibility of cherrypicking reported results in ASR industry audits. Without a more nuanced consideration of each step of an ASR audit, ASR audits could enshrine harmful assumptions in ASR and Artificial Intelligence services more broadly \cite{sloane2022silicon, harvey2024cadaver, andrews2024reanimation}, and exacerbate harms to already-marginalized speech communities or create downstream problems of ``audit-washing'' \cite{gmfusAuditWashingAccountability}. 

We argue that there are three core pitfalls that must be addressed to improve standard auditing procedures for ASR models -- (1) the absence of robust text standardization experiments which rarely take into account community preference for ground truth; (2) the lack of nuanced considerations of demographic subgroups and audio file characteristics affecting ASR performance; and (3) the overemphasis on a single gold standard evaluation metric of ASR services. To ameliorate these three problems, we lay out our proposed community-based auditing procedures in Figure~\ref{fig:audit_pipeline}. First, different text standardization methods should be explored---accounting for a range of decisions such as whether to include contractions, filler words, stutters, etc.---and auditors should report variance in performance metrics across these standardization methods. This is especially pertinent since different communities may have different preferences about how they would like their speech to be transcribed. Second, ASR performance studies should attempt to study the heterogeneity within demographic groups. Groups are not monolithic; for instance, not all African Americans speak African American English \cite{koenecke2020racial}, which affects ASR performance in relation to subgroup identity. Other influences on ASR performance include intersectional features like accented speech combined with age, and other covariates like background noise (linked to socioeconomic factors \cite{casey2017race, dale2015socioeconomic}) and pauses in speech (correlated with age and medical conditions \cite{geng2022speaker, romana2024fluencybank}). Third, ASR auditors should report on a suite of metrics rather than relying solely on a single standard metric---Word Error Rate (WER)---which fails to capture semantic meaning or nuanced errors such as hallucinated transcriptions \cite{koenecke2024careless}.

To address these issues, our study focuses on ASR performance of American English spoken by individuals with aphasia, a language disorder that is caused by damage to language-related regions in the brain or stroke and that affects communication \cite{benson1996aphasia}. Aphasia affects about 0.06\% of the population \cite{Code2011} and significantly impacts patient's lives, causing challenges in returning to work \cite{graham2011aphasia} and in social interactions \cite{fama2022subjective}. We center aphasia patients because they are intersectionally disadvantaged: typically over 50 years of age \cite{nguy2022representation}, underrepresented in speech datasets, and often relying on ASR technology \cite{wallacewells2024fetterman}. We use data from AphasiaBank \cite{aphasiabank}, a repository of spontaneous speech interviews with both individuals with aphasia, and control speakers without speech, language, or hearing disorders. We also survey members of the aphasia community to understand their experiences with ASR services, and their preferences for ASR transcriptions. Our ASR services of interest include six popular commercial and open-source services developed by Amazon, AssemblyAI, Google, Microsoft, OpenAI, and Rev AI. Amazon, Google and Microsoft were selected for being leading major tech companies and have been widely used in prior audits \cite{koenecke2020racial, Zhao2024, HidalgoLopez2023}, while AssemblyAI, OpenAI, and Rev AI represent prominent third-party services frequently utilized for specialized ASR applications, also employed in previous audits \cite{kuhn2024measuring, attanasio2024multilingual, heuser2024quantification}. 

Using the aforementioned standard auditing procedure, we first compare performance across all six ASR services between individuals with aphasia and a control group of American English speakers without language disorders, finding significantly worse WERs for aphasia speakers. We then more closely examine each of the three pitfalls identified, and perform additional analyses to address each pitfall. 
%outlined earlier: (1) identifying text standardization approaches that align with aphasia speakers' preferences, (2) conditioning on relevant speaker covariates beyond high-level demographics, and (3) quantifying hallucination rates as an alternative metric to WER. 
Taken together, our results---on average---yield similar results to those of a standard audit. However, our additional analyses yield more robust insights on the factors leading to ASR underperformance for speakers with aphasia.

