\section{Results}\label{sec2}

\subsection{Standard Auditing Procedure}\label{sec2.1}
Following standard audit procedures to quantify the difference in ASR performance among aphasia speakers and control group speakers (see Appendix %\autoref{sec:asr-transcript-collection}
for details on running ASR services), we examined the performance of six leading ASR services using the Word Error Rate (WER) metric. WER is defined as the number of word changes between the ASR-generated transcription as compared to the ground truth transcription, normalized by the number of words in the ground truth transcription. We calculated the WER across 6,185 audio files from AphasiaBank (comprised of 4,342 aphasia and 1,843 control audio files spoken by 321 aphasia and 216 control speakers, respectively). 

We found that ASR transcriptions of aphasia speakers consistently yielded worse WERs (6-10 percentage points higher) than control speakers across all six ASR services  (see Appendix \autoref{wer_unmatched_weighted})% Table \ref{tab:WER_rate}
, revealing statistically significant (p-value $<0.001$) disparities. Microsoft performed the worst (WERs of 17\% for aphasia, 9\% for control) while RevAI performed the best (WERs of 12\% for aphasia, 6\% for control)\footnote{We calculate WER for aphasia or control groups by aggregating all text within each group (so word substitutions, insertions, and deletions across audio files are combined as if they were all from a single audio file), as is standard per~\cite{Ji2023}. For unweighted WER averages (i.e., calculating the WER for each audio file and then averaging within group, per~\cite{koenecke2020racial}), see Appendix \autoref{tab:WER_rate}. %\ref{tab:WER_rate}. 
The unweighted WER averages are consistently 4-9 percentage points higher than the weighted WER averages; regardless of weighting aggregation, the difference in WER between control and aphasia groups is consistent (a difference of 6-12 percentage points in both cases).\label{fn:wer_avg}}. These findings were consistent when subsetting our analysis to 2,448 propensity-matched audio files (see Section \ref{method:matching}), balancing age, gender, years of education, and race/ethnicity (see Appendix \autoref{tab:wer_test_results}). %\ref{tab:wer_test_results})

While these results highlight the underperformance of ASR services for aphasia speakers, this analysis treats them as a monolith, ignoring variability in speech types and transcription preferences while focusing solely on WER. Accordingly, we address three key pitfalls. In the following three subsections, we (1) challenge the assumptions made in the idea of nominal ground-truthing by experimenting with different data pre-processing approaches for text standardization; (2) reveal performance disparities for participants with different aphasia types and demographics; and (3) go beyond the use of WER as a single metric and suggest possibilities for additional evaluation metrics of ASR performance.

\subsection{Pitfall 1: Nominal Text Standardization Method}\label{sec:pitfall-standardization}
The first pitfall in current auditing studies occurs in the often-overlooked data pre-processing stage: `cleaning' the ASR-generated transcriptions and ground truth text before calculating the WER.  There is precedent for ASR providers adopting the cleaning protocol norms from academia: for example, the open-source package for text standardization used by OpenAI's Whisper~\cite{radford2023robust} was based on cleaning steps from an earlier academic study \cite{koenecke2020racial}. Across prior ASR audit studies, the concept of data cleaning is rarely, if at all, defined; it nearly always involves some form of standardization that lowercases all text and removes punctuation \cite{Ji2023}, but can also involve standardizing contractions (e.g., ensuring that `gonna' and `going to' are considered as equivalent transcriptions), removing filler words (such as `um' or `uh'), etc.

However, the default choices in standardization vary across different ASR services, meaning special attention must be paid to ensure outputs across services are comparable. In \autoref{tab:groundtruth-step}, we provide an example focusing on one sample sentence uttered by an aphasia speaker in our corpus. When transcribing this specific sentence, we exemplify five different methods of standardization, each of which is valid and defensible.\footnote{Existing industry distinctions between ``clean'' and ``verbatim'' transcripts are broad~\cite{amberscript_transcription_guidelines}, e.g., respectively,  as either ``grammatically correct'' or focused more on notation for external audio or paralinguistic features complying with  consumer demands.} These correspond to whether one would transcribe fillers, fragments, repeated words, or repeated phrases -- or none of the above. Depending on which standardization method is chosen and applied to both the ground truth and ASR-generated transcription, the ensuing WER could change (since the number of words in the ground truth---i.e., the denominator of WER---could be different). This could, in turn, change the results of any audit. The default settings of a given ASR service may dictate the transcription cleaning used by the majority of downstream users. For example, while most ASR services automatically remove filler words from transcriptions, Amazon AWS does not. AWS also does not remove word fragments (when only the beginning of a word---but not the full word---is uttered), unlike most other ASR services. While most ASR services transcribe verbally repeated words, OpenAI Whisper may automatically remove these repetitions. Concerningly, while some services offer parameters for text standardization within the API, many do not. Some newer variants of ASR services even focus specifically on improving ``verbatim transcriptions,'' which ensure that filler words as originally stated are correctly transcribed~\cite{wagner2024crisperwhisper} rather than potentially arbitrarily removed. %A table summarizing these differences appears in \autoref{tab:groundtruth-step}.

