In the following two subsections, we raise two specific concerns regarding standardization in audit studies. First, different speech communities have different preferences for how their speech transcription should be standardized, but these users are rarely consulted by ASR services regarding these preferences. This results in standardizations---both in real-life ASR use-cases, and in audit studies---that do not reflect how users want to be transcribed. Second, publicly reported audit studies rarely report results with experiments that vary standardization methods, making it difficult to determine whether results are robust to different standardization preferences across users. 


\subsubsection{Community-based Preferences for Standardization}

Researchers in technology design have long advocated for the inclusion of all stakeholders in the decision-making process of designs~\cite{kensing1998participatory,lee2020human,delgado2023participatory}. However, ASR audit practices tend to overlook stakeholder preferences of ASR outputs, even though they directly impact the lives of diverse users. For an aphasia speaker who says ``H- h- he he wanted to, they were um'', they may prefer to be transcribed as the speech was intended, and without evidence of stuttering, e.g.: ``He wanted to, they were.'' The latter transcription is meaning-preserving, though it may not be as faithful to the original audio file. While existing ASR service-based standardization defaults are not necessarily unfaithful to the original audio, the choice of the extent to which transcriptions are standardized should be made with different stakeholder preferences in mind.

Situated in aphasia speakers' everyday usage of ASR, we consider their preferences for output standardization as guidance for our auditing analysis, stipulating that their preferred ASR transcription style ought to constitute the ground truth. This community-first approach in AI auditing is particularly relevant in the context of ASR used by people with disabilities, as per the ``nothing about us without us'' principle that emphasizes community--led design and assessment \cite{charlton1998nothing, costanza2023nothing}.  We performed in-depth qualitative interviews and quantitative online surveys on recruited participants with aphasia; details on recruitment and sample size\footnote{Our sample size of six participants, while small, follows the practices of recent papers similarly exploring fairness in technology with only 10-13 interviewees~\cite{deng2022exploring, gu2021understanding, deng2023understanding,caine2016local}, and is further restricted given the low incidence rate of aphasia (estimated at 0.06\%~\cite{Code2011}).} are in the Methods Section. %~\ref{method:recruitment}.
Of the six participants, five indicated that ASR services currently perform only ``moderately well'' for their speech. All six participants preferred the most extensively-cleaned text standardization option provided (i.e., ``He wanted to, they were having a ball'' in \autoref{tab:groundtruth-step}---removing filler words, fragments, and word and phrase repetitions). Qualitatively, these participants were in strong agreement that the most condensed version of the transcription was that which most accurately reflected their intended speech.

We further note that standardization preferences could vary by the purpose of ASR application. While aphasia patients using ASR services for everyday tasks may prefer a standardization approach as described above, medical doctors diagnosing aphasia or speech pathologists tracking rehabilitation progress may instead prefer verbatim transcriptions that include repeated words or phrases (as such details are crucial for monitoring medical conditions \cite{le2018automatic, fraser2013automatic, fraser2014automated, stemberger2020phonetic}). Even in casual conversational settings without atypical speech patterns involved, repetition could indicate strong emphasis depending on the associated paralinguistic features, and stylistic preferences could vary due to specific sociolinguistic features such as dialects \cite{heuser2024quantification}. This raises a critical question that should be addressed in audits: \textit{whose standardization preferences form the basis of the ground truth?}

\subsubsection{Robustness by Standardization Types}

Based on the surveyed aphasia speakers' responses aligning with most extensively-cleaned text standardization, our previously-presented ``standard'' audit results adhered to this standardization method (removing filler words, fragments, and phrase repetitions). We further propose that audits additionally showcase the variance in  results across different levels of text standardization. We present such results in \autoref{fig:groundtruth_comparison}, showing that WER for aphasia speakers improves significantly when removing filler words, and again when removing fragments (per nonparametric pairwise tests showing statistically significant WER reductions for all six ASR services). As such, choice of standardization method can alter both the reported WER, and the rank ordering of a performance audit among the six ASR services. Robustness checks for the control group are reported in Appendix \autoref{fig:groundtruth_comparison_control}.

Strikingly, in half of the ASR services tested, there was no significant difference in average WER for aphasia speakers between removing fillers and fragments, and additionally removing repeated words. In these cases, aligning with aphasia speaker preferences for text standardization (removing word-level stutters) does not yield a significantly different WER for some ASR services. In all standardization approaches among aphasia patients, average WERs are within 2 percentage points of each other (findings are comparable among control patients, see Appendix \autoref{tab:average_wer_standardization}). This exemplifies that the choice to apply a community-based standardization---legitimized by ASR users, as opposed to externally-imposed by the ASR service themselves---is not necessarily at odds with achieving a high-performing WER. 

