\subsection{Pitfall 2: Analysis of Overly-Broad User Groups} \label{sec:pitfall-demographic}

While it may seem straightforward to calculate average WER differences between the aphasia and control groups, as done in Pitfall 1, the second pitfall in ASR auditing studies lies in the tendency to perform only aggregate-level comparisons (e.g., comparing a treatment group to a control group). These analyses---often performed by broad demographic group---can ignore two important considerations discussed in the following two subsections: first, that treatment groups are not monolithic, and second, that other important covariates may play a role in differential ASR performance.

\subsubsection{Aphasia Subgroup Analysis}

We begin by introducing different subcategories of aphasia based on clinical impressions. At a high level, aphasia can be categorized as either \emph{fluent} aphasia or \emph{non-fluent} aphasia \cite{le2023aphasia}. People who experience fluent aphasia ``can speak in sentences that sound like normal speech, but some of the words are made-up words or have some sounds that are not correct'' \cite{le2023aphasia}. Meanwhile, patients who experience non-fluent aphasia often ``struggle to get words out, omit words, and speak in very short sentences'' \cite{le2023aphasia}. At a more granular level based on the Boston Diagnostic Aphasia Examination (BDAE)~\cite{goodglass2001bdae}, aphasia can be categorized into eight different types (each of which is considered either fluent or non-fluent). These aphasia types range from \emph{Global aphasia} (a non-fluent aphasia broadly considered the most severe) to \emph{Anomic aphasia} (a fluent aphasia with milder symptoms) \cite{knopman2011regional}. More details are provided in Appendix \autoref{aphasia-category}. 

While a standard audit might present results similar to those described in our initial results comparing all aphasia speakers to all control speakers, we instead propose examining aphasia speakers more granularly to account for their heterogeneity. \autoref{fig:merged_figure1} shows average WER performance by ASR service for control speakers (from 1,843 audio files) as compared to aphasia speakers by subtype: non-fluent and fluent (from 1,437 and 2,346 audio files, respectively). We find that disaggregating aphasia speakers showcases that non-fluent aphasia has significantly worse (higher) average WER relative to fluent aphasia (WER of 0.21 versus 0.13, respectively, with $p<0.001$ for all ASR services). In fact, some fluent aphasia speakers have WER performance much closer to control speakers than to less-fluent aphasia speakers, though both fluent and non-fluent aphasia speakers have significantly worse (higher) average WER relative to the control group ($p<0.001$ for all six ASR services). This finding is robust across WER aggregation method (\autoref{fn:wer_avg}) and propensity-matched subsets (see Appendix \autoref{fig:aphasia_type_fluency_matched_weighted}). %\ref{method:matching}).

We further disaggregate ASR performance by more granular aphasia classifications per the BDAE in \autoref{aphasia-type1-wer}. Of the eight categories of aphasia, five are represented in the AphasiaBank data. First, we again find that WER performance can differ significantly within the set of aphasia patients: the Broca and Conduction aphasia groups have significantly higher WER than the Anomic aphasia group ($p<0.001$). Second, we again find that all types of aphasia exhibit worse (higher) average WERs compared to the control group (all pairwise p-values $<0.05$, across all ASR services). Among them, Global aphasia speakers have the highest average WER at 30.5\%, followed by Broca's aphasia at 21.5\%, Wernicke's aphasia at 17.8\%, Conduction aphasia at 15.9\%, and Anomic aphasia at 11.7\%---consistent with the ordering of fluency expected from clinical impressions per Appendix \autoref{aphasia-category}. 

Overall, these findings point towards the need to disaggregate broad groups to better understand heterogeneity within groups. This is especially true for audit studies that generate rank orderings of ASR services based on WER performance, such as leaderboards\footnote{Leaderboards are public rankings that compare the performance of different models or services on standardized benchmarks. For example, the Hugging Face Open ASR Leaderboard (\url{https://huggingface.co/spaces/hf-audio/open_asr_leaderboard})}. These rankings (e.g., each vertical column in \autoref{aphasia-type1-wer}) change depending on what subtype of aphasia is being evaluated in the audit. For example, while OpenAI's Whisper performs third-best out of six ASR services on Anomic aphasia (a fluent aphasia with mild symptoms), Whisper performs the worst on Global aphasia (the most severe non-fluent aphasia). As such, it is imperative that audits consider reporting evaluations on more granular subgroups, which can help inform third-party users  about which might be the best ASR service for them to use.

While this section focuses on granularity of aphasia type, there are many other ways to study  subgroups, especially relating to intersectionality. Prior work on the intersection of race and gender has found, for example, that ASR services perform worse for Black men and best for white women~\cite{koenecke2020racial}. We similarly find that women consistently have better performance than men in the control group, and that the WER gender gap widens for fluent aphasia speakers, and is widest for non-fluent aphasia speakers. See Appendix \autoref{fig:WER_average_gender_unmatched_unweighted} and \autoref{fig:WER_average_race_unmatched_unweighted} for additional analyses on gender and race intersected with aphasia type. Additional to such intersectional evaluations, we now discuss relevant measured characteristics to consider.
